WEBVTT

00:00:19.840 --> 00:00:25.150
I promised you four years ago that I would never lie to you,

00:00:25.150 --> 00:00:28.320
so I can't stand here tonight and say it doesn't hurt.

00:00:30.480 --> 00:00:34.320
About an hour ago, I called Governor Reagan in California,

00:00:34.320 --> 00:00:37.530
and I congratulated him for a fine victory.

00:00:37.530 --> 00:00:39.270
I look forward to working closely with him.

00:00:39.270 --> 00:00:42.270
All his life, he believed that if you worked hard enough

00:00:42.270 --> 00:00:46.060
at it, understood the issues, mastered information,

00:00:46.060 --> 00:00:48.960
then you would come out first.

00:00:48.960 --> 00:00:52.660
I said to him, it must have been hard to turn over the keys

00:00:52.660 --> 00:00:54.180
to Ronald Reagan.

00:00:54.180 --> 00:00:56.520
And he said, you don't know how hard it was.

00:00:56.520 --> 00:01:00.000
On January 1st,

00:01:00.000 --> 00:01:08.120
February 20, 1981, after one of the most humiliating defeats in American political history, President

00:01:08.120 --> 00:01:18.050
Jimmy Carter returned home to Plains, Georgia, to what he called an altogether new, unwanted,

00:01:18.050 --> 00:01:22.380
and potentially empty life.

00:01:22.380 --> 00:01:30.000
He really was better than I was when we came home because I was so depressed about it that

00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:40.860
always trying to prop me up. Four years before he had stunned the nation. Going from total

00:01:40.860 --> 00:01:45.420
anonymity to being president of the United States in less than 12 books is unprecedented

00:01:45.420 --> 00:01:50.920
in American history. If it weren't for the country looking for something in 76, Carter

00:01:50.920 --> 00:02:00.000
could never have gotten elected. He offered a biography of what we wanted to hear, a farmer,

00:02:00.000 --> 00:02:05.680
values, planes. And he carried that message through. It was the right message at the right

00:02:05.680 --> 00:02:13.410
time. Our commitment to human rights must be absolute. He had promised a new beginning,

00:02:13.730 --> 00:02:21.980
to heal the wounds of Watergate and Vietnam, a government as good and decent and compassionate

00:02:21.980 --> 00:02:30.000
as the American people. But events would overwhelm him. An energy

00:02:30.000 --> 00:02:41.680
crisis, inflation, an Islamic revolution, and 53 Americans held hostage, 444 days.

00:02:43.500 --> 00:02:48.900
Carter came to be regarded as a good and decent man who was in over his head.

00:02:50.190 --> 00:02:54.460
He's a very, very smart man and very well-intentioned.

00:02:54.460 --> 00:03:00.000
But feel, feel is very, very important in politics, especially in a president.

00:03:00.510 --> 00:03:03.640
and Carter just didn't have very much of it.

00:03:03.640 --> 00:03:06.360
- What he had was a moral ideology,

00:03:06.360 --> 00:03:09.500
and the issues where he proved successful,

00:03:09.500 --> 00:03:12.890
the Panama Canal treaties, the Human Rights Crusade,

00:03:12.890 --> 00:03:14.480
peace in the Middle East,

00:03:14.480 --> 00:03:17.560
those were issues where his moral ideology guided him.

00:03:17.560 --> 00:03:21.410
- In a nation that was proud of hard work.

00:03:21.410 --> 00:03:24.050
- Carter was one of the more exasperating men

00:03:24.050 --> 00:03:28.010
ever to claim the White House, one journalist said.

00:03:28.010 --> 00:03:30.000
His tenacity so admirable

00:03:30.090 --> 00:03:33.810
could shift to stubbornness, his religious faith

00:03:33.810 --> 00:03:36.630
to self-righteousness.

00:03:36.630 --> 00:03:41.550
His brilliant mind could be bound up by intricate details.

00:03:41.550 --> 00:03:44.960
Many times, the one argument that I would find

00:03:44.960 --> 00:03:48.120
would ruin a person's case is when he'd say,

00:03:48.120 --> 00:03:50.140
this is good for you politically.

00:03:50.140 --> 00:03:51.810
He didn't want to hear that.

00:03:51.810 --> 00:03:53.090
He didn't want to think that way,

00:03:53.090 --> 00:03:54.790
and he didn't want his staff to think that way.

00:03:54.790 --> 00:03:58.840
He wanted to know what's right.

00:03:58.840 --> 00:04:00.000
This is one of the most highly

00:04:00.000 --> 00:04:01.950
ambitious people you'll ever meet.

00:04:01.950 --> 00:04:04.190
I mean, you don't make it from claims Georgia

00:04:04.190 --> 00:04:07.560
to the White House just on charm.

00:04:07.560 --> 00:04:10.090
But what makes him complex is he's got

00:04:10.090 --> 00:04:12.480
that kind of hubris and arrogance

00:04:12.480 --> 00:04:15.240
and also this Christian humbleness.

00:04:15.240 --> 00:04:18.440
And that's the battle he's constantly finding himself in.

00:04:45.580 --> 00:04:51.700
As a child, my greatest ambition was to be valuable around the farm and to please my father.

00:04:52.390 --> 00:04:55.130
Jimmy Carter wrote of his boyhood in rural Georgia.

00:04:56.780 --> 00:05:00.000
He was the center of my life and the focus of my agriculture.

00:05:00.000 --> 00:05:09.960
I can't believe that Jimmy Carter ever felt lost in the sense that he didn't know where

00:05:09.960 --> 00:05:13.500
his place was in the world.

00:05:13.500 --> 00:05:20.510
And a lot of that comes from his father, who not only was a well-respected, powerful figure

00:05:20.510 --> 00:05:25.590
in the community, but I think had a real sense of who he was.

00:05:25.590 --> 00:05:30.000
And that certitude and self-confidence was something that his son

00:05:30.000 --> 00:05:32.000
I think absorbed unconsciously

00:05:32.880 --> 00:05:38.110
By the standards of Southwest, Georgia

00:05:38.630 --> 00:05:43.550
Earl Carter presided over a small empire a staunch

00:05:44.110 --> 00:05:46.110
segregationist he owned some

00:05:46.450 --> 00:05:50.780
350 acres of land where he planted corn cotton and peanuts

00:05:51.780 --> 00:06:00.000
employing more than 200 workers at harvest time five sharecropper families who depended on him for their survival

00:06:00.000 --> 00:06:03.440
lived year-round at his farm in archery.

00:06:03.440 --> 00:06:07.210
Earl was the boss in archery.

00:06:07.210 --> 00:06:09.320
The workers were all black.

00:06:09.320 --> 00:06:12.940
The maids who did the cooking and took care of Jimmy were black.

00:06:12.940 --> 00:06:15.740
And at the top of the system was Earl Carter.

00:06:15.740 --> 00:06:22.940
From a position of privilege, Earl's children, Jimmy, Gloria, and Ruth,

00:06:22.940 --> 00:06:27.140
became acquainted with the ways of the Jim Crow South.

00:06:30.160 --> 00:06:43.220
More than anyone else in my family, even my own father, I understood the plight of the black families because I lived so much among them, Carter later wrote.

00:06:43.220 --> 00:06:51.830
He often ate and slept in the homes of his black neighbors and played with their children.

00:06:51.830 --> 00:06:59.640
The interesting thing about the South is that we played together black and white when we were seven, eight, nine, ten.

00:07:01.210 --> 00:07:09.250
But then when you got to be a teenager, all of a sudden, segregation set in.

00:07:09.250 --> 00:07:15.380
One day, my friends and I approached a gate, Carter would later recall.

00:07:17.170 --> 00:07:20.770
To my surprise, they stepped back to let me go through first.

00:07:23.610 --> 00:07:26.410
It was a small act, but a deeply symbolic one.

00:07:28.890 --> 00:07:30.000
Things were never the same.

00:07:30.000 --> 00:07:48.270
between them and me a strong memory in my mind is coming home and my mother not

00:07:48.270 --> 00:07:54.650
being there he wrote there's a very deep tradition in southern society of the

00:07:54.650 --> 00:08:00.000
caretaker mother figure who is responsible not only for her family but outside of

00:08:00.000 --> 00:08:07.210
it as well. Well, those people exist in almost every southern rural community. But Miss Lillian

00:08:07.210 --> 00:08:09.290
took it a step further than that.

00:08:09.290 --> 00:08:17.530
Carter's mother, Lillian, was an avid reader, loved traveling, and was known to enjoy a sip

00:08:17.530 --> 00:08:25.090
of bourbon. She put in long hours as a nurse at a nearby hospital and devoted much of her

00:08:25.090 --> 00:08:30.000
free time to helping sick neighbors, regardless of race.

00:08:30.380 --> 00:08:33.380
She got paid in chickens and vegetables and that kind of things

00:08:33.380 --> 00:08:35.380
because she really helped and felt called

00:08:35.380 --> 00:08:37.380
to help those that had less than her.

00:08:37.380 --> 00:08:40.380
And I think she instilled that in all of her children.

00:08:40.380 --> 00:08:43.800
She was the only person in Plains

00:08:43.800 --> 00:08:46.800
who would take up for Abraham Lincoln if he was ever brought up.

00:08:46.800 --> 00:08:49.140
Today is unbelievable to think about that,

00:08:49.140 --> 00:08:51.140
but back then it was just a way of life,

00:08:51.140 --> 00:08:53.140
and we never thought anything --

00:08:53.140 --> 00:08:55.510
we never thought it was really wrong.

00:08:55.510 --> 00:09:00.000
Lillian set for Jimmy the example

00:09:00.000 --> 00:09:06.270
of service to others. Earl put the steel in his character.

00:09:06.270 --> 00:09:13.850
He was very demanding. He expected his children to be the very best, and in some ways they

00:09:13.850 --> 00:09:17.990
all had that built into them.

00:09:17.990 --> 00:09:23.540
I never remember him saying good job when I did my best to fulfill his orders, Jimmy

00:09:23.540 --> 00:09:30.000
later said. The punishments he administered remain vivid in my memory.

00:09:30.000 --> 00:09:47.200
a short distance from the Carter farm was Plains Georgia population 600 the only place for miles

00:09:47.200 --> 00:10:00.000
to get a cup of coffee a haircut buy or sell goods it is the place Jimmy Carter

00:10:00.090 --> 00:10:06.570
always called home. Where as a child he went to the all-white Baptist Church on

00:10:06.570 --> 00:10:13.230
Sundays and where he attended the all-white public school. Everybody knew

00:10:13.230 --> 00:10:17.870
that he was special. He was somebody different, smarter than, worked harder

00:10:17.870 --> 00:10:23.930
than, did more than, ceaselessly working at improving himself even as a child.

00:10:23.930 --> 00:10:30.000
Jimmy made all A's. He played basketball and joined the

00:10:30.000 --> 00:10:39.070
Booklovers Club, read Shakespeare's King Lear, Ben-Hur, The Hunchback of Notre Dame.

00:10:39.070 --> 00:10:42.570
He dreamed of joining the Navy.

00:10:42.570 --> 00:10:48.950
His uncle Tom Gordy had excited his imagination with tales of adventures and postcards and

00:10:48.950 --> 00:10:54.570
gifts from exotic, faraway places.

00:10:54.570 --> 00:11:00.000
Earl encouraged Jimmy to pursue his dream.

00:11:00.000 --> 00:11:05.760
many young southern men got the polish, got the education that would make them a

00:11:05.760 --> 00:11:11.930
part of either the local elite or the national elite. Jimmy reviewed the strict

00:11:11.930 --> 00:11:16.920
requirements of the US Naval Academy at Annapolis and worried he wasn't good

00:11:16.920 --> 00:11:22.160
enough. He thought his feet were flat and rolled them over coke bottles to

00:11:22.160 --> 00:11:30.000
strengthen the arches. He thought he was too thin and went on a banana diet. He even went to a

00:11:30.000 --> 00:11:37.790
local college for two years to study the required courses. He just wouldn't quit, Jimmy's uncle

00:11:37.790 --> 00:11:50.670
Alton would later say. That boy just wouldn't give up on anything. In June 1943, at age 18, the farm

00:11:50.670 --> 00:12:00.000
boy from Plains was admitted to the U.S. Naval Academy at Annapolis, the first Carter ever to leave Georgia to pursue

00:12:00.000 --> 00:12:01.700
a higher education.

00:12:01.700 --> 00:12:14.310
Jimmy's sister Ruth was my best friend, and she had a picture of him on the wall in her

00:12:14.310 --> 00:12:17.180
bedroom.

00:12:17.180 --> 00:12:22.220
I just thought he was the most handsome young man I had ever seen.

00:12:22.220 --> 00:12:27.220
One day I confessed to her that I wish she would let me take that photograph home, because

00:12:27.220 --> 00:12:29.800
I just thought I had fallen in love with Jimmy Carter.

00:12:32.040 --> 00:12:36.040
Rosalind Smith was shy, a dedicated student,

00:12:36.040 --> 00:12:40.840
read the Bible daily, and went to church on Sundays.

00:12:40.840 --> 00:12:43.280
Her mother once described her as a girl

00:12:43.280 --> 00:12:49.490
who could wear a white dress all day and keep it clean.

00:12:49.490 --> 00:12:50.840
She was very bright.

00:12:50.840 --> 00:12:52.130
She was a reader.

00:12:52.130 --> 00:12:53.840
She liked to look at maps.

00:12:53.840 --> 00:12:56.900
She was always interested in seeing the world.

00:12:56.900 --> 00:12:59.570
And she always wanted to get away.

00:13:01.880 --> 00:13:06.000
I went to a meeting at the church and I was standing outside and

00:13:06.930 --> 00:13:11.950
Jimmy drove up with Ruth and her boyfriend got out of the car and came up and asked me to go to the movie

00:13:12.150 --> 00:13:17.120
He kissed me goodbye. I was thrilled to death and then we started corresponding and

00:13:18.130 --> 00:13:22.210
By the time Christmas came I was swept off my feet

00:13:22.920 --> 00:13:30.000
One months after his graduation from a national

00:13:30.680 --> 00:13:44.030
Jimmy and Rosalyn were married. She was 18. He was 21. The Carters began married life in Norfolk, Virginia

00:13:44.610 --> 00:13:50.850
where the Navy lieutenant first reported for duty. The children arrived in quick succession.

00:13:51.320 --> 00:13:58.420
Jack one year after their wedding, James Earl or Chip less than three years later in Hawaii and

00:14:00.000 --> 00:14:03.370
And a third, Jeff, born in New London, Connecticut.

00:14:06.210 --> 00:14:13.440
With her husband away at sea, Rosalind found herself alone and in charge of all the affairs of the Carter household.

00:14:15.950 --> 00:14:18.930
I felt inadequate and very lonely, she later said.

00:14:20.370 --> 00:14:23.310
Sometimes I cried, though I didn't let Jimmy know.

00:14:23.310 --> 00:14:30.000
He has no patience with tears, thinking instead that one makes the best of whatever situation.

00:14:30.000 --> 00:14:37.530
and with a smile I've learned to be very independent I could take care of myself

00:14:37.530 --> 00:14:42.130
and the baby and and do things that I never dreamed I would be able to do

00:14:42.130 --> 00:14:50.290
alone two years after joining the Navy Ensign Carter was accepted into the

00:14:50.290 --> 00:14:55.890
submarine service it was a way to advance rapidly in a highly competitive

00:14:55.890 --> 00:15:00.000
environment the military was everything for

00:15:00.000 --> 00:15:01.000
It's his training.

00:15:01.000 --> 00:15:03.790
He's never a minute late for anything.

00:15:03.790 --> 00:15:05.790
Punctuality means everything.

00:15:05.790 --> 00:15:09.970
His sense of order, there's no sense of a mess around Jimmy Carter.

00:15:09.970 --> 00:15:14.170
It's a certain kind of person that works in a submarine.

00:15:14.170 --> 00:15:17.130
It takes a kind of mental discipline.

00:15:17.130 --> 00:15:21.070
While the rest of the officers lingered after dinner

00:15:21.070 --> 00:15:24.070
or settled in for a long game of bridge or poker,

00:15:24.070 --> 00:15:28.330
his shipmates remembered Carter would read a book,

00:15:28.330 --> 00:15:30.000
solve a sonar problem,

00:15:30.000 --> 00:15:37.010
always something constructive I mastered the assignments that I had Carter

00:15:37.010 --> 00:15:42.920
would say of his naval career got the best fitness reports and I never put in

00:15:42.920 --> 00:15:47.780
for anything that I did not get she's coming up out of the deep the sea wolf

00:15:47.780 --> 00:15:52.720
after six years in the service lieutenant Carter earned one of the most

00:15:52.720 --> 00:16:00.000
coveted posts in the Navy senior officer of the USS Seawolf on the vanguard

00:16:00.000 --> 00:16:08.090
of America's nuclear defense program he always had one of the best positions in

00:16:08.090 --> 00:16:14.640
the Navy and I think gave him a lot of confidence that he could do whatever he

00:16:14.640 --> 00:16:22.960
wanted to do in 1953 less than a year after he began duty on the seawolf

00:16:22.960 --> 00:16:30.000
Carter received a message from home his father had cancer and was not

00:16:30.000 --> 00:16:49.300
expected to live long years had passed since Carter left planes for a career in

00:16:49.300 --> 00:17:00.000
the Navy visits home had been rare father and son had grown distant as he sat

00:17:00.030 --> 00:17:04.750
by Earl's bedside Jimmy discovered a side of his father he'd never seen

00:17:04.750 --> 00:17:13.070
before our long conversations were interrupted by a stream of visitors black

00:17:13.070 --> 00:17:19.160
and white Carter later wrote a surprising number wanted to recount how

00:17:19.160 --> 00:17:25.220
my father's personal influence and many secret acts of generosity had affected

00:17:25.220 --> 00:17:30.000
their lives he saw that he had really built a

00:17:30.000 --> 00:17:31.620
a community around himself.

00:17:31.620 --> 00:17:36.120
A lot of people liked him and came to see him when he was sick

00:17:36.120 --> 00:17:38.460
and when he died, came to his funeral.

00:17:38.460 --> 00:17:40.980
And what Jimmy realized, he didn't

00:17:40.980 --> 00:17:43.080
have a community for himself.

00:17:43.080 --> 00:17:45.670
He actually said to me, you know,

00:17:45.670 --> 00:17:48.840
he said, I wondered at that time if I died,

00:17:48.840 --> 00:17:51.480
how many people would come to my funeral

00:17:51.480 --> 00:17:54.590
or how many people would care if I died?

00:17:54.590 --> 00:18:00.000
And I think it made him at a fairly fundamental level examine

00:18:00.000 --> 00:18:03.310
what life is all about.

00:18:03.310 --> 00:18:05.970
- Duty also weighed on Carter.

00:18:05.970 --> 00:18:08.900
Miss Lillian had no interest in the business

00:18:08.900 --> 00:18:11.970
and Ruth and Gloria had married.

00:18:11.970 --> 00:18:15.690
His brother Billy, just 16, was mad as hell

00:18:15.690 --> 00:18:22.910
when told his older brother would be stepping in.

00:18:22.910 --> 00:18:25.760
- He was a shining star in the U.S. Navy

00:18:25.760 --> 00:18:28.300
who could have gone very, very far.

00:18:28.300 --> 00:18:29.640
He dropped all that.

00:18:30.000 --> 00:18:34.320
emulate his father to take over his father's business i don't think there's any higher tribute

00:18:34.320 --> 00:18:38.800
a son could make to his father than to say now that you're dead daddy i want to stand in your

00:18:38.800 --> 00:18:51.520
shoes when jimmy told rosalyn she was furious she almost quit me he later said mom was kind

00:18:51.520 --> 00:18:55.610
of disappointed to be going back to plains that she had worked a good bit of her life

00:18:55.610 --> 00:19:00.000
to get out of there and uh they were going back to take over a business that wasn't

00:19:00.000 --> 00:19:05.390
Roseland had finally got out of that Flyspec village

00:19:05.490 --> 00:19:08.190
and had gotten to see the bright lights in big cities.

00:19:08.290 --> 00:19:11.130
Imagine being based in Hawaii where you get a Pacific breeze

00:19:11.230 --> 00:19:14.170
and palm trees and the smell of the Orient in the air.

00:19:14.270 --> 00:19:16.580
And now you're back in the suffocating,

00:19:16.680 --> 00:19:19.560
mosquito-plagued humidity of Plains, Georgia.

00:19:19.660 --> 00:19:22.290
She pleaded with him not to go.

00:19:22.390 --> 00:19:27.660
She had seen a very nice life ahead of them,

00:19:27.760 --> 00:19:30.000
and then he wanted to give that opportunity.

00:19:30.000 --> 00:19:34.540
all up and go back and become a peanut farmer and she was just really angry and

00:19:34.540 --> 00:19:49.750
she literally did not talk to him the whole way back to Plains.

00:19:49.750 --> 00:19:56.100
Pouted for about a year, not really, but it was just the total mother and wife.

00:19:56.100 --> 00:19:58.960
It was tough for a while.

00:20:01.630 --> 00:20:03.140
We loved it.

00:20:03.140 --> 00:20:08.530
Plains is a place where at six or seven, eight years old, you can go off by yourself.

00:20:08.530 --> 00:20:12.730
We spend every afternoon after school in the woods, playing, hiding seat and building forts

00:20:12.730 --> 00:20:14.790
and fishing and hiking and that kind of thing.

00:20:14.790 --> 00:20:18.240
It was just a great way to grow up.

00:20:18.240 --> 00:20:30.000
A year after their return home, the Carters were thrust into the

00:20:30.030 --> 00:20:38.470
turmoil sweeping across the south. In 1954, the Supreme Court outlawed segregation in

00:20:38.470 --> 00:20:46.080
public schools. White southerners organized into white citizens councils vowed to resist.

00:20:46.080 --> 00:20:53.050
Jimmy was approached by one of the prominent businessmen asking him to join the white citizens

00:20:53.050 --> 00:20:58.440
council. And he told him that it only cost $5 to join and that he would be glad to pay

00:20:58.440 --> 00:20:59.280
his dues.

00:21:00.570 --> 00:21:04.090
I think Jimmy told him he could flash his money down the john.

00:21:04.090 --> 00:21:06.380
But anyway, Jimmy refused to do that,

00:21:06.380 --> 00:21:08.900
and we lost some customers.

00:21:08.900 --> 00:21:14.090
I won't say it was a profile in full courage,

00:21:14.090 --> 00:21:16.490
but it was not an act of discretion.

00:21:16.490 --> 00:21:18.770
You had to carefully think about it.

00:21:18.770 --> 00:21:20.770
And it required, at the very least,

00:21:20.770 --> 00:21:24.010
a kind of independence of thought, and in some respects,

00:21:24.010 --> 00:21:27.970
a kind of courage to say, no, this I won't do.

00:21:36.590 --> 00:21:41.470
Carter applied all his energies to peanuts.

00:21:41.470 --> 00:21:44.540
He was always experimenting, Rosalyn later wrote,

00:21:44.540 --> 00:21:47.470
trying new things, dreaming up something else

00:21:47.470 --> 00:21:51.530
he wanted to do.

00:21:51.530 --> 00:21:58.640
As the business expanded, he turned to Rosalyn for help.

00:21:58.640 --> 00:22:00.000
Mom is not really the time.

00:22:00.000 --> 00:22:02.550
to join the Stitch and Chat and to sit around

00:22:02.650 --> 00:22:04.230
and be content with that.

00:22:04.260 --> 00:22:08.450
Part of their uniqueness is that they're partners in everything.

00:22:08.540 --> 00:22:11.280
And I think a lot of that started back then

00:22:11.380 --> 00:22:12.880
to make her a part of what was happening

00:22:12.980 --> 00:22:15.520
so that she would really have something to be proud of.

00:22:15.690 --> 00:22:18.600
He asked me to come and keep the office.

00:22:18.640 --> 00:22:22.140
And I had a friend who taught an accounting course

00:22:22.240 --> 00:22:23.820
in the vocational technical school,

00:22:23.920 --> 00:22:25.690
and she gave me a set of accounting books.

00:22:25.790 --> 00:22:27.070
I began to study accounting.

00:22:27.170 --> 00:22:28.870
I began to keep the books.

00:22:28.970 --> 00:22:30.000
And it was not too long.

00:22:30.000 --> 00:22:39.820
before I knew actually as much or more about the business on paper than he did.

00:22:39.820 --> 00:22:44.680
I started working there when I was nine. We worked in the warehouse during

00:22:44.680 --> 00:22:49.140
peanut season. Peanut season was a very heavy time. Sometimes worked 50 hours

00:22:49.140 --> 00:22:55.090
straight. I think that he worked hard. He tried to instill it in his children. He

00:22:55.090 --> 00:22:58.070
obeyed his father, jumped when he spoke. We did the same thing.

00:23:02.460 --> 00:23:07.980
"I had to admit I was enjoying life," Rosalyn later said.

00:23:07.980 --> 00:23:10.920
The Carters went fishing, played golf,

00:23:10.920 --> 00:23:15.650
took frequent vacations.

00:23:15.650 --> 00:23:19.600
Jimmy served on the Sumter County Board of Education,

00:23:19.600 --> 00:23:23.080
taught Sunday school at the Plains Baptist Church,

00:23:23.080 --> 00:23:26.660
was scoutmaster, vice president of the Lions Club.

00:23:30.000 --> 00:23:35.130
But he had come to the point of boredom, Rosalynn remembered.

00:23:35.130 --> 00:23:42.320
And one weekday morning in 1962, he got up and put on his Sunday pants.

00:23:42.320 --> 00:23:43.320
I was really shocked.

00:23:43.320 --> 00:23:51.640
I had no idea he was thinking about running for the state senate.

00:23:51.640 --> 00:23:58.930
The campaign that launched Jimmy Carter's political career lasted all of 15 days.

00:23:58.930 --> 00:24:00.000
There was no money.

00:24:00.000 --> 00:24:11.910
and no staff, only family and friends and his own determination, though he would always

00:24:11.910 --> 00:24:14.790
play the reluctant politician.

00:24:14.790 --> 00:24:20.830
Even by his first campaign, Carter was no stranger to politics.

00:24:20.830 --> 00:24:26.000
Politics was something he lived and breathed from the time he was a child, a kind of weekly

00:24:26.000 --> 00:24:30.000
daily even during election season interaction, barbecues.

00:24:30.510 --> 00:24:34.580
You gathered on the county courthouse grounds for speeches.

00:24:34.580 --> 00:24:37.020
He talks about going to rallies with his father

00:24:37.020 --> 00:24:39.410
and remembering them very well.

00:24:39.410 --> 00:24:43.290
And I think he came to see politics as something not alien,

00:24:43.290 --> 00:24:45.890
not something he had to make a decision to do,

00:24:45.890 --> 00:24:48.710
but was almost natural.

00:24:48.710 --> 00:24:52.730
"I received a startling education," he said,

00:24:52.730 --> 00:24:55.510
"one that set the tone for my future career."

00:25:02.750 --> 00:25:08.380
Whitman County historically had been run by a man named Joe Hurst.

00:25:08.380 --> 00:25:14.280
And Joe was not atypical for many, many small counties in the state, poorer counties.

00:25:14.280 --> 00:25:22.240
You had one person who was a political power, who just, in effect, kind of ran the county.

00:25:22.240 --> 00:25:30.000
Hurst was used to getting what he wanted, and in 1962, he wanted another Democrat, Homer

00:25:30.480 --> 00:25:33.580
to be elected senator.

00:25:33.580 --> 00:25:35.800
- The ballot box was a liquor box

00:25:35.800 --> 00:25:39.810
that had been taken and cold-cut in the top of it

00:25:39.810 --> 00:25:42.710
so that you put your ballot over in there after you.

00:25:42.710 --> 00:25:44.710
And it was set up on the counter,

00:25:44.710 --> 00:25:48.350
and you had to come up and mark your ballot right next to it

00:25:48.350 --> 00:25:51.930
with Joe and a bunch of his crowd watching, you know,

00:25:51.930 --> 00:25:54.620
while you're doing it.

00:25:54.620 --> 00:25:56.380
- Fraud was rampant.

00:25:56.380 --> 00:25:59.320
Voters were threatened, ballots destroyed.

00:26:00.830 --> 00:26:05.940
Joe Hurst even stuffed ballots of dead voters into the old crow box.

00:26:05.940 --> 00:26:12.490
That evening, when the votes were counted, Jimmy Carter had lost.

00:26:12.490 --> 00:26:16.590
He decided to contest the election.

00:26:16.590 --> 00:26:22.540
By all accounts, even allowing for a certain hyperbole in the memory of Mr. Carter,

00:26:22.540 --> 00:26:29.220
this required an extraordinary kind of doggedness and just keeping at it, keeping at it, and not giving up.

00:26:31.280 --> 00:26:38.380
Carter appealed to newspapers, filed for injunctions, took affidavits from voters.

00:26:38.380 --> 00:26:47.310
Miss Lillian kept saying, "Jimmy is so naive, so naive."

00:26:47.310 --> 00:26:52.500
There were threats against the Carters. Jimmy was followed.

00:26:52.500 --> 00:26:59.730
A stranger came by the Carter warehouse and warned Rosalind that the last time anyone had crossed Joe Hurst,

00:27:00.000 --> 00:27:06.880
business had burned down I was constantly scared she later said Jimmy

00:27:06.880 --> 00:27:14.300
was frightened too two weeks later a local judge agreed to hear Carter's case

00:27:14.300 --> 00:27:21.250
when it came time to open that box and recount it right there rolled up into a

00:27:21.250 --> 00:27:29.250
ball while these ballots and judge crow was a funny fella he chewed tobacco

00:27:30.000 --> 00:27:34.460
And he had just cut off a little piece of tobacco and put in his mouth, and he was kind

00:27:34.460 --> 00:27:36.280
of putting it around.

00:27:36.280 --> 00:27:43.500
And I was, I saw that, and I saw him cut his eyes, stop chewing, and then go back to chewing

00:27:43.500 --> 00:27:44.500
and sit back.

00:27:44.500 --> 00:27:49.250
And right then is when I knew we had that thing won.

00:27:49.250 --> 00:27:55.870
On January 14th, 1963, the morning after the traditional whiskey and barbecued wild

00:27:55.870 --> 00:28:00.000
hog dinner, Jimmy Carter was sworn in as a member of

00:28:00.000 --> 00:28:06.840
the Georgia Senate. He was one of 89 new legislators joining the Georgia

00:28:06.840 --> 00:28:13.070
Assembly, many of them determined to change the old ways of Georgia politics.

00:28:13.070 --> 00:28:19.030
I had the good fortune of being the first black to be elected to the General

00:28:19.030 --> 00:28:24.750
Assembly of Georgia in a hundred years. Carter was one of those persons who came

00:28:24.750 --> 00:28:30.000
to the Senate at that time and he was not a leader

00:28:30.000 --> 00:28:39.950
of the Senate. He was quiet, he was effective, he was deliberate, and he made no ways.

00:28:41.950 --> 00:28:49.040
Carter opposed special interests and sweetheart deals. He worked hard and read every bill,

00:28:49.580 --> 00:28:52.480
staying away from drinking sprees and poker games.

00:28:53.620 --> 00:29:00.000
In the last session of the state Senate in his last year there, I was standing in the back of

00:29:00.000 --> 00:29:05.790
the Senate chamber with him and the lieutenant governor was going on and on

00:29:05.790 --> 00:29:11.060
and on and it was bedlam like the last day and Jimmy said if I were lieutenant

00:29:11.060 --> 00:29:16.070
governor this wouldn't be happening and I thought oh he's really enjoying this

00:29:16.070 --> 00:29:24.250
in 1966 after two terms in the Georgia Senate Jimmy Carter

00:29:24.250 --> 00:29:30.000
jumped into the race for governor of Georgia he ran well behind Arch Senate

00:29:30.000 --> 00:29:35.600
segregationist Lester Maddox, famous for wielding an axe handle to keep blacks away from his

00:29:35.600 --> 00:29:42.760
chicken restaurant. Carter left his younger brother Billy in charge of the business while

00:29:42.760 --> 00:29:54.780
the rest of the family went on the road. I think I had $25 a week for expenses to

00:29:54.780 --> 00:30:00.000
eat on and I had a gas credit card and we came in every Saturday night and

00:30:00.030 --> 00:30:02.610
told what we had been doing.

00:30:02.610 --> 00:30:04.430
It was a real education for all of us,

00:30:04.430 --> 00:30:08.060
and we were doing it as a family.

00:30:08.060 --> 00:30:09.620
- I would come home and ask him questions

00:30:09.620 --> 00:30:12.160
that people asked me while I was campaigning,

00:30:12.160 --> 00:30:13.620
and I didn't know the answers to,

00:30:13.620 --> 00:30:14.660
and he would give me the answers

00:30:14.660 --> 00:30:17.340
so I could go back out and talk about issues.

00:30:17.340 --> 00:30:21.380
He had confidence in me to do the things that I needed to do.

00:30:21.380 --> 00:30:24.430
- Carter promised better schools,

00:30:24.430 --> 00:30:27.900
better hospitals, better roads,

00:30:27.900 --> 00:30:29.730
and a more competent government.

00:30:31.920 --> 00:30:35.890
It is hard to hear Senator Carter talk about state government

00:30:35.890 --> 00:30:40.540
and not be impressed by his integrity, one reporter wrote.

00:30:40.540 --> 00:30:43.420
You're not going to turn the apple cart upside down,

00:30:43.420 --> 00:30:44.910
but you're going to bring changes.

00:30:44.910 --> 00:30:47.850
You're going to bring improvements in the South.

00:30:47.850 --> 00:30:52.740
And you're going to do it by applying good, sound business

00:30:52.740 --> 00:30:55.420
techniques to everything from the way

00:30:55.420 --> 00:30:57.820
you run your public institutions

00:30:57.820 --> 00:30:59.760
to the way you run your government.

00:31:00.570 --> 00:31:06.110
CARTER TOOK HIS MESSAGE TO EVERY CORNER OF GEORGIA: "WE NEVER STOPPED," ROSALIND

00:31:06.110 --> 00:31:13.480
RECALLED, "NO MATTER WHAT." BY ELECTION DAY, HE WAS CLOSING IN ON THE LEAD.

00:31:13.480 --> 00:31:18.510
WE WENT TO BED, THINKING WE WERE GOING TO WIN, HAD GOTTEN UP AND GONE TO SCHOOL THE

00:31:18.510 --> 00:31:23.530
NEXT DAY BEING CONGRATULATED ABOUT MY FATHER WINNING THE PRIMARY. AND THEN BILLY CAME

00:31:23.530 --> 00:31:27.300
TO THE -- ABOUT 2:00 IN THE AFTERNOON AND TOLD US THAT LESS DRAMATICS HAD BEAT US BY

00:31:27.300 --> 00:31:30.000
LESS THAN A HALF OF A PERCENTAGE POINT. SO IT WAS VERY DISHEARTMENT.

00:31:32.080 --> 00:31:35.120
"We all felt sick," Rosalind recalled.

00:31:35.120 --> 00:31:42.940
"We were $66,000 in debt, and Jimmy had lost 22 pounds."

00:31:42.940 --> 00:31:48.490
After all the miles traveled, the handshakes, the long days,

00:31:48.490 --> 00:31:51.410
Jimmy Carter was right back where he started

00:31:51.410 --> 00:31:54.930
when he first ran for the Senate in 1962.

00:32:02.200 --> 00:32:08.740
weeks later with the law still fresh in his mind jimmy went for a walk with his sister ruth

00:32:08.740 --> 00:32:17.440
an evangelical christian and spiritual healer all of his life he had been a church-going christian

00:32:17.440 --> 00:32:24.360
but now felt that his faith had been superficial we are both baptists he said

00:32:24.360 --> 00:32:30.000
but what is it that you have that i haven't got total confusion

00:32:30.000 --> 00:32:37.440
Commitment, she replied, "I belong to Jesus, everything I am."

00:32:37.440 --> 00:32:40.430
Ruth, he answered, "That's what I want."

00:32:40.430 --> 00:32:46.610
At that point, he decided that he'd always put Christ in his life first and politics

00:32:46.610 --> 00:32:50.830
second, but that's been a struggle for him because politics is the ego and Christ is

00:32:50.830 --> 00:32:51.830
the humbleness.

00:32:51.830 --> 00:32:58.870
The born-again Christian traveled north to blighted neighborhoods in Pennsylvania

00:32:58.870 --> 00:33:00.000
and Massachusetts.

00:33:01.150 --> 00:33:06.030
as a personal witness for Jesus Christ.

00:33:06.030 --> 00:33:09.470
He would go door to door, getting people to witness

00:33:09.470 --> 00:33:11.330
Christ take Jesus into their lives.

00:33:11.330 --> 00:33:13.370
I mean, can you imagine 10 years later,

00:33:13.370 --> 00:33:14.930
this man's president of the United States,

00:33:14.930 --> 00:33:16.830
and he's banging on doors, asking people,

00:33:16.830 --> 00:33:17.710
"Do you want a Bible?

00:33:17.710 --> 00:33:20.510
Will you take God in your life?"

00:33:20.510 --> 00:33:24.020
He wanted to understand theology.

00:33:24.020 --> 00:33:26.560
And so he began reading a lot of theologians

00:33:26.560 --> 00:33:30.000
and began to craft for himself a political

00:33:30.000 --> 00:33:37.350
of a political theology that was compatible with his own personality.

00:33:37.350 --> 00:33:44.500
Carter found guidance in the writings of theologian Reinhold Niebuhr.

00:33:44.500 --> 00:33:51.070
Niebuhr said, "The sad duty of politics is to bring justice to a sinful world."

00:33:51.070 --> 00:33:53.770
A Christian has to get involved in politics.

00:33:53.770 --> 00:34:00.000
He has to soil his hands as a politician or in an immoral society

00:34:00.000 --> 00:34:02.810
to improve it.

00:34:02.810 --> 00:34:09.460
Niebuhr taught him that there is good and evil in the world and that politics is corrupt,

00:34:09.460 --> 00:34:14.690
but it's honorable as long as you kept your heart pure and your sense of morality pure.

00:34:14.690 --> 00:34:22.690
"I believe God wants me to be the best politician I can be," he said.

00:34:30.090 --> 00:34:41.000
In 1970, with renewed fervor, Carter ran for governor of Georgia a second time.

00:34:41.000 --> 00:34:46.480
I've been campaigning almost 18 hours a day without stopping for eight months.

00:34:46.480 --> 00:34:50.480
I've seen almost every tractor shift in Georgia. I've been in almost every store.

00:34:50.480 --> 00:34:56.480
It was just kind of an obsession. He had lost, so we had to win, and we worked as hard as we could.

00:34:56.480 --> 00:35:00.000
It would not be the only thing to do

00:35:00.000 --> 00:35:08.700
amateur run of 1966 but a well coordinated effort carter brought in two southwest georgia boys

00:35:09.310 --> 00:35:15.300
jody powell as his personal assistant and hamilton jordan to manage the campaign

00:35:16.940 --> 00:35:21.740
advertising man gerald rafschoon would handle the media

00:35:21.740 --> 00:35:29.250
bert lance a banker from calhoun played the role of advisor so it was a tough tough campaign

00:35:30.000 --> 00:35:31.880
and there were many who thought that Carter

00:35:31.880 --> 00:35:34.520
could not possibly win.

00:35:34.520 --> 00:35:37.560
- Carter's rival for the Democratic nomination,

00:35:37.560 --> 00:35:41.430
Carl Sanders, enjoyed a commanding 20% lead.

00:35:43.080 --> 00:35:46.400
He had the backing of the Atlanta business establishment

00:35:46.400 --> 00:35:48.960
and the support of African Americans,

00:35:48.960 --> 00:35:50.340
voting in greater numbers

00:35:50.340 --> 00:35:54.950
since the Voting Rights Act of 1965.

00:35:54.950 --> 00:35:57.640
Carter went after Sanders with a vengeance.

00:35:57.640 --> 00:36:00.000
- Some candidates in this governor's race have large candidates

00:36:00.090 --> 00:36:07.180
contributions behind them. Big money asking big favors. Jimmy Carter only has

00:36:07.180 --> 00:36:11.880
the people of Georgia. Carter portrayed Sanders as a tool of the Atlanta

00:36:11.880 --> 00:36:16.380
business establishment and himself as a hard-working everyman. No wonder Jimmy

00:36:16.380 --> 00:36:20.580
Carter has a special understanding of the problems facing everyone who works

00:36:20.580 --> 00:36:23.760
for a living. Isn't it time somebody spoke up for you?

00:36:23.760 --> 00:36:30.000
He wanted to appeal to the large middle-class blue-collar type, predominantly

00:36:30.000 --> 00:36:37.470
white and most of these people are going to be segregationist he courted the

00:36:37.470 --> 00:36:42.030
racist vote there were some radio ads that he ran in 1970 he said that I'm

00:36:42.030 --> 00:36:47.310
like Sanders I am NOT trying to get the and he sort of slid over when it was

00:36:47.310 --> 00:36:52.890
blocker black vote but it sort of meant the same thing part of himself was not a

00:36:52.890 --> 00:37:00.000
segregationist in 1970 but he did say things that the segregationists wanted to hear

00:37:00.000 --> 00:37:02.370
He was opposed to busing.

00:37:02.470 --> 00:37:04.870
He was in favor of private schools.

00:37:04.970 --> 00:37:07.730
He said that he would invite segregationist

00:37:07.830 --> 00:37:11.670
Governor George Wallace to come to Georgia to give a speech.

00:37:11.770 --> 00:37:17.690
The only solace that we got and received

00:37:17.790 --> 00:37:20.980
was the fact that in private meetings,

00:37:21.080 --> 00:37:25.710
he convinced us that if he was given an opportunity,

00:37:25.810 --> 00:37:27.500
he would make things better.

00:37:27.600 --> 00:37:30.000
He always come up with this question.

00:37:30.000 --> 00:37:36.570
question of trust trust me I believe in doing the right thing if you aren't

00:37:36.570 --> 00:37:41.110
really trying to accomplish good moral ends you may have to be a low-life

00:37:41.110 --> 00:37:45.390
politician to get there and he didn't probably like doing it that much but he

00:37:45.390 --> 00:38:00.000
was willing to do it on January 12 1971 Jimmy Carter age four

00:38:00.000 --> 00:38:03.100
46 was sworn in as governor of georgia

00:38:05.300 --> 00:38:10.180
In his inaugural address he revealed his true feelings on race

00:38:11.360 --> 00:38:13.880
That the time for racial

00:38:15.170 --> 00:38:20.660
Discrimination is over no cool

00:38:22.720 --> 00:38:30.000
Weak or black person should ever have to bear the additional burden of being deprived of the opportunity

00:38:30.000 --> 00:38:37.740
opportunity of an education, a job, or a simple justice.

00:38:37.740 --> 00:38:40.380
We were extremely pleased.

00:38:40.380 --> 00:38:44.000
Many of the white segregations were displeased.

00:38:44.000 --> 00:38:50.850
And I'm convinced that those people that supported him would not have supported him if they had

00:38:50.850 --> 00:38:54.550
thought that he would have made that statement.

00:38:54.550 --> 00:39:00.000
I do remember reading his inaugural address and thinking, this is wonderful.

00:39:00.000 --> 00:39:05.110
out a governor in a deep south state who's stating emphatically not just that it's time

00:39:05.110 --> 00:39:11.170
to accept change, but that it's time to really move far beyond that and end all forms of

00:39:11.170 --> 00:39:12.170
discrimination.

00:39:12.170 --> 00:39:18.720
And I suddenly saw him as part of this new generation of southern politicians who were

00:39:18.720 --> 00:39:27.010
moving beyond the divisions of racial politics in the 1950s and '60s.

00:39:27.010 --> 00:39:30.000
The Carters moved into the brand new governor's mansion.

00:39:30.000 --> 00:39:33.750
on 18 acres in Atlanta's poshest district.

00:39:34.260 --> 00:39:36.200
This is the first time our family has really been together

00:39:36.200 --> 00:39:37.880
for the last four years.

00:39:38.840 --> 00:39:40.540
Our oldest boy has just gotten back from the Navy,

00:39:40.540 --> 00:39:43.230
and I've been campaigning for four years.

00:39:43.760 --> 00:39:46.440
So we're looking forward to living as a family again.

00:39:46.560 --> 00:39:48.840
Mrs. Carter, have you had any special problems?

00:39:49.360 --> 00:39:50.040
No, not really.

00:39:50.240 --> 00:39:54.170
We packed the clothes in the car last night,

00:39:54.470 --> 00:39:57.720
and really the only furniture we had to bring was one sofa,

00:39:57.820 --> 00:40:00.000
which is just a favorite old sofa that my children love.

00:40:00.000 --> 00:40:03.000
Going from Plains, Georgia to the governor's mansion

00:40:03.000 --> 00:40:05.000
was much harder for me than going from the governor's

00:40:05.000 --> 00:40:07.300
mansion to the White House.

00:40:07.300 --> 00:40:11.420
I had never, ever been in the governor's mansion.

00:40:11.420 --> 00:40:12.760
When Jimmy was in the state senate,

00:40:12.760 --> 00:40:15.690
I didn't come to Atlanta because I was working at home.

00:40:15.690 --> 00:40:19.660
I was not part of the Atlanta political community.

00:40:19.660 --> 00:40:22.910
It was a really difficult experience.

00:40:22.910 --> 00:40:24.990
She had to learn her own voice, how to project,

00:40:24.990 --> 00:40:26.990
how to make a speech, how to win people over,

00:40:26.990 --> 00:40:29.090
how to deal with legislators on her issues.

00:40:30.280 --> 00:40:33.530
And she had to learn how to do all that.

00:40:33.530 --> 00:40:37.730
The people of Georgia came to meet the new first family

00:40:37.730 --> 00:40:42.640
and fell in love with Amy, the Carter's three-year-old daughter.

00:40:42.640 --> 00:40:46.240
The public identifies with a small child,

00:40:46.240 --> 00:40:48.240
and Carter understood that.

00:40:48.240 --> 00:40:51.690
And they kept Amy in the limelight.

00:40:51.690 --> 00:40:53.070
It made him human.

00:40:53.070 --> 00:40:55.610
He could be a successful politician, a successful

00:40:55.610 --> 00:41:00.000
governor, and a successful father, all at

00:41:00.000 --> 00:41:00.930
same time.

00:41:00.930 --> 00:41:07.890
Carter appointed an unprecedented number of women and African-Americans, stimulated

00:41:07.890 --> 00:41:14.300
foreign investment, reformed the state's criminal justice and mental health systems.

00:41:14.300 --> 00:41:20.590
I see unfair taxes and government waste, and I see runaway spending.

00:41:20.590 --> 00:41:26.850
The centerpiece of his agenda was a radical plan to streamline state government, with

00:41:26.850 --> 00:41:30.000
savings at every level.

00:41:30.000 --> 00:41:32.200
Everybody had to pay for their own lunch, you know.

00:41:32.200 --> 00:41:34.970
We had to put $2 into the kitty.

00:41:34.970 --> 00:41:37.610
Mary Beasley, who was his secretary at the time,

00:41:37.610 --> 00:41:40.060
would ask you what you wanted.

00:41:40.060 --> 00:41:42.700
So you felt honored to be able to go in and spend money

00:41:42.700 --> 00:41:45.900
for a dried out sandwich.

00:41:45.900 --> 00:41:48.400
The governor's proposal to slash the number

00:41:48.400 --> 00:41:51.920
of government agencies provoked outrage.

00:41:51.920 --> 00:41:55.360
I welcome the confrontation with heads of departments.

00:41:55.360 --> 00:41:57.850
You know, I'm willing to fight with anybody

00:41:57.850 --> 00:41:59.730
who opposes a recommendation.

00:42:00.060 --> 00:42:01.060
- That would be helpful.

00:42:01.060 --> 00:42:04.330
- I saw a completely different side of Carter.

00:42:04.330 --> 00:42:06.970
In the Senate, he was not assertive.

00:42:06.970 --> 00:42:09.090
As governor, he was assertive.

00:42:09.090 --> 00:42:10.550
He knew where he wanted to go,

00:42:10.550 --> 00:42:13.400
and he knew the direction he wanted to go in,

00:42:13.400 --> 00:42:18.260
and he wanted complete compliance.

00:42:18.260 --> 00:42:19.960
- He's fighting for total submission

00:42:19.960 --> 00:42:22.920
and total control of the legislators,

00:42:22.920 --> 00:42:25.720
and he's willing to use 100 million or 200 million

00:42:25.720 --> 00:42:26.740
or whatever it is.

00:42:26.740 --> 00:42:29.860
- Jim is a character as such

00:42:30.410 --> 00:42:37.780
that he wants to get things done he wants them done and he has a tendency I

00:42:37.780 --> 00:42:44.170
think to run roughshod over anything that stands in his way we need to

00:42:44.170 --> 00:42:51.310
remember who pays his taxes and who pays our salary he had a tendency to take his

00:42:51.310 --> 00:43:00.000
case to the people and then try to force the legislature to follow him he never as

00:43:00.000 --> 00:43:06.890
Governor broke what I think was an unfortunate habit of seeing personal politics as a kind of,

00:43:07.090 --> 00:43:10.570
that is with other politicians, as a kind of nuisance, something that had to be done

00:43:10.570 --> 00:43:15.530
because you had to talk to these people. He never developed the interest in or really

00:43:15.530 --> 00:43:21.090
particularly good skills at working with individuals who may have disagreed with him.

00:43:21.090 --> 00:43:30.000
Pay more than $312.50 a month. By the time his reorganization

00:43:30.000 --> 00:43:36.610
bill reached the Senate floor, Carter had alienated most of the assembly. But his bill

00:43:36.610 --> 00:43:40.310
squeaked through by a handful of votes.

00:43:40.310 --> 00:43:48.140
As I told the committee up there, he reminds me of a South Georgia turtle who's been blocked

00:43:48.140 --> 00:43:53.230
by a log. And he just keeps pushing, pushing, pushing straight ahead. He doesn't go around

00:43:53.230 --> 00:44:00.000
until he finally gets a soft spot in the log and right on through he goes. He's a man of

00:44:00.000 --> 00:44:16.660
Election season 1972, Jimmy Carter extended his hospitality to Democratic hopefuls.

00:44:16.660 --> 00:44:23.600
Barely two years in the governor's mansion, he already had his eye on the White House.

00:44:23.600 --> 00:44:30.000
Every Democrat running for office came to Georgia, and every single one of them, Deb

00:44:30.000 --> 00:44:32.740
to come and stay with him at the governor's mansion

00:44:32.740 --> 00:44:37.520
and realized that they were just people like him.

00:44:37.520 --> 00:44:40.940
- That July, Carter led the Georgia delegation

00:44:40.940 --> 00:44:43.610
to the Democratic National Convention,

00:44:43.610 --> 00:44:46.200
hoping for the second spot on the ticket.

00:44:46.200 --> 00:44:47.200
- I'm over here in the box

00:44:47.200 --> 00:44:49.440
and I really can't tell what's going on so much,

00:44:49.440 --> 00:44:51.540
but Jimmy comes over from the floor

00:44:51.540 --> 00:44:53.920
and kind of briefs me every once in a while.

00:44:53.920 --> 00:44:56.020
As you said, it's the first time I've ever been to a convention

00:44:56.020 --> 00:44:57.960
and I'm just so excited about it.

00:44:57.960 --> 00:45:00.000
- I remember at the end of the McGovern's

00:45:00.000 --> 00:45:05.590
speech at three o'clock in the morning. Hamilton, Jordan and I were walking away from the convention

00:45:05.590 --> 00:45:16.440
hall. I said, you know, if Ed Muskie, Hubert Humphrey, Terry Sanford, Scoop Jackson, George

00:45:16.440 --> 00:45:22.690
Wallace, Ted Kennedy can run for president, Jimmy could run for president. And then, of

00:45:22.690 --> 00:45:26.850
course, we said, and if these guys who are running these campaigns, like we met the people

00:45:26.850 --> 00:45:30.000
in the McGovern campaign, can run a campaign for president.

00:45:30.000 --> 00:45:31.870
Hell, we could do that.

00:45:31.870 --> 00:45:33.210
- I called Ruth.

00:45:33.210 --> 00:45:35.510
I said, "Jimmy's going to run for..."

00:45:35.510 --> 00:45:36.770
I couldn't even say the word.

00:45:36.770 --> 00:45:40.970
It was so unreal to me.

00:45:40.970 --> 00:45:44.830
- I'm one of 15 or 20 people in the country

00:45:44.830 --> 00:45:46.750
who work actively in the Democratic Party

00:45:46.750 --> 00:45:50.110
who have been mentioned for a place on the ticket next year.

00:45:50.110 --> 00:45:52.710
- Carter's timing was perfect.

00:45:52.710 --> 00:45:53.930
For the next two years,

00:45:53.930 --> 00:45:56.910
Americans would be gripped by the Watergate scandal.

00:45:58.100 --> 00:46:00.000
Disillusioned with politics,

00:46:00.000 --> 00:46:01.500
They were ready for a change.

00:46:01.500 --> 00:46:08.700
It was 1970, early '74.

00:46:08.700 --> 00:46:11.740
I went over to see him one night.

00:46:11.740 --> 00:46:13.040
Rosen was out of town.

00:46:13.040 --> 00:46:17.070
I went over to the governor's mansion, and I said, "Let's just talk about what the themes

00:46:17.070 --> 00:46:18.920
would be."

00:46:18.920 --> 00:46:27.410
And he took a yellow pad and he wrote, "Fairness, not from Washington, not a lawyer, Southerner,

00:46:27.410 --> 00:46:28.860
religious."

00:46:28.860 --> 00:46:30.000
These things that were coming.

00:46:30.000 --> 00:46:30.740
from Carter

00:46:30.740 --> 00:46:31.760
were the themes

00:46:31.760 --> 00:46:32.380
of the campaign.

00:46:33.300 --> 00:46:34.000
Jimmy who?

00:46:34.500 --> 00:46:35.260
Jimmy Carter?

00:46:35.790 --> 00:46:36.230
I don't know

00:46:36.230 --> 00:46:36.870
who he is.

00:46:37.430 --> 00:46:38.150
Jimmy Carter's

00:46:38.150 --> 00:46:38.770
special person.

00:46:39.660 --> 00:46:41.040
Carter officially

00:46:41.040 --> 00:46:41.860
announced his

00:46:41.860 --> 00:46:42.880
candidacy in

00:46:42.880 --> 00:46:44.660
December 1974.

00:46:45.520 --> 00:46:46.700
The one-term

00:46:46.700 --> 00:46:47.500
Southern governor

00:46:47.500 --> 00:46:48.740
was a long shot.

00:46:49.820 --> 00:46:50.460
Nobody knew him.

00:46:50.540 --> 00:46:51.060
It was like picking

00:46:51.060 --> 00:46:51.620
a name out of

00:46:51.620 --> 00:46:52.160
the phone book.

00:46:52.830 --> 00:46:53.850
I mean, it takes

00:46:53.850 --> 00:46:54.690
a bit of hubris

00:46:54.690 --> 00:46:55.310
to think you're

00:46:55.310 --> 00:46:56.190
the best person

00:46:56.190 --> 00:46:57.210
to be the president

00:46:57.210 --> 00:46:58.050
of the United States

00:46:58.050 --> 00:46:58.690
because you were

00:46:58.690 --> 00:46:59.250
a one-term

00:46:59.250 --> 00:47:00.000
governor of Georgia.

00:47:00.480 --> 00:47:03.080
Once and for all, why not the best?

00:47:03.590 --> 00:47:05.940
It's a kind of arrogance run amok.

00:47:06.040 --> 00:47:07.600
His name was Jenny Carter.

00:47:08.500 --> 00:47:14.630
They want to see us once again have a nation that's as good and honest and decent and truthful

00:47:14.630 --> 00:47:24.150
and competent and compassionate and as filled with love as are the American people.

00:47:24.490 --> 00:47:28.520
At that time, character was a monumental issue.

00:47:29.310 --> 00:47:30.000
Country.

00:47:30.000 --> 00:47:36.100
had been through a horrible time and Jimmy Carter represented honesty and decency I'll never tell a

00:47:36.100 --> 00:47:43.230
lie I'll never make a misleading statement I'll never betray the confidence that any of you has

00:47:43.230 --> 00:47:47.870
in me Lyndon Johnson lied to us about Vietnam Richard Nixon lied to us about Watergate he's

00:47:47.870 --> 00:48:00.000
saying you know I'm not one of those turkeys who's messing things up up there his campaign strategy was simple

00:48:00.960 --> 00:48:07.570
run early and run hard before any other candidates even announced

00:48:07.570 --> 00:48:11.230
carter had traveled more than 50 000 miles

00:48:11.230 --> 00:48:16.940
visited 37 states and delivered more than 200 speeches

00:48:16.940 --> 00:48:20.290
he was a wonderful speaker before small groups

00:48:20.290 --> 00:48:26.470
he would get up and talk without notes with extraordinary passion

00:48:26.470 --> 00:48:30.000
almost like a preacher really having the

00:48:30.000 --> 00:48:38.310
with him. It was a grassroots effort financed on a shoestring. We had all these

00:48:38.310 --> 00:48:41.450
stepping stones we had to do. We had to qualify for federal matching funds by a

00:48:41.450 --> 00:48:44.690
certain point, and we accomplished every one of them. Every time you accomplished one,

00:48:44.690 --> 00:48:48.750
it gave you more and more confidence. We had our boys out, we had Aunt Sissy out,

00:48:48.750 --> 00:48:53.390
we had his mother, all going in different directions. At one point in the

00:48:53.390 --> 00:48:58.260
presidential campaign, we had 11 family members in 11 different states at the same time.

00:49:01.470 --> 00:49:09.470
first test came in January 1976 with no delegates at stake other candidates

00:49:09.470 --> 00:49:14.920
wrote off the Iowa caucuses but Carter saw them as a way to surface early and

00:49:14.920 --> 00:49:22.570
gain the attention of the press Iowa put Carter on the political map and gave

00:49:22.570 --> 00:49:30.000
him momentum heading into a field of better-known Democrats Mo Udall

00:49:30.000 --> 00:49:36.210
Birch Bayh, Sergeant Shriver, in the all-important New Hampshire primary.

00:49:36.210 --> 00:49:43.730
We had almost a month between the Iowa caucuses and the New Hampshire primary, which gave

00:49:43.730 --> 00:49:50.800
us time to build on the win, both in terms of recognition and coverage and in terms of

00:49:50.800 --> 00:49:57.290
raising just enough money to make it through New Hampshire and have a bit left over.

00:49:57.290 --> 00:50:00.000
Campaign volunteers from Georgia, the Peanut Brigade.

00:50:00.480 --> 00:50:02.920
descended on the Granite State.

00:50:02.920 --> 00:50:06.760
- We were out every day knocking on doors.

00:50:06.760 --> 00:50:10.320
We knocked on 60,000 doors in New Hampshire.

00:50:10.320 --> 00:50:12.860
That was probably almost every Democratic household

00:50:12.860 --> 00:50:14.760
that we could identify in the whole state.

00:50:14.760 --> 00:50:16.990
- Hello. - Yes, yes.

00:50:16.990 --> 00:50:18.150
- I'm Dr. Perdina.

00:50:18.150 --> 00:50:21.380
- You'd say, Mrs. Smith, my name is Betty Pope,

00:50:21.380 --> 00:50:23.600
and I'm from America's Georgia.

00:50:23.600 --> 00:50:26.580
And if Mrs. Smith was there with her dog,

00:50:26.580 --> 00:50:28.750
I would remember that this beautiful lab

00:50:28.750 --> 00:50:30.000
came to the door with her.

00:50:30.570 --> 00:50:34.170
So I'd make a note, and I'd talk to her a little bit about Jimmy,

00:50:34.170 --> 00:50:36.520
and often it was, "Have you ever met him?"

00:50:36.520 --> 00:50:38.820
And of course, that's why we were there.

00:50:38.820 --> 00:50:40.420
So we did get our name out,

00:50:40.420 --> 00:50:44.870
and I think that we surprised America when he won.

00:50:44.870 --> 00:50:47.170
Jimmy Carter took a long lead tonight

00:50:47.170 --> 00:50:50.070
in the race for the Democratic presidential nomination.

00:50:50.070 --> 00:50:53.530
He won the New Hampshire primary.

00:50:53.530 --> 00:50:58.770
I remember when we couldn't find a microphone.

00:51:01.660 --> 00:51:05.390
The next crucial contest was Florida.

00:51:05.390 --> 00:51:10.770
The leading contender, George Wallace, the former governor of Alabama, was an outspoken

00:51:10.770 --> 00:51:14.880
segregationist who had become a liability to the Democratic Party.

00:51:14.880 --> 00:51:22.170
We'd run a northerner that was right on civil rights, and George Wallace would steal a third

00:51:22.170 --> 00:51:25.610
of our vote, and we couldn't get elected.

00:51:25.610 --> 00:51:30.000
Here came along a man from the South with very good civil rights.

00:51:30.000 --> 00:51:38.580
credentials who just might be able to handle George Wallace as a native son

00:51:38.580 --> 00:51:44.440
Carter could appeal to white voters he also had the support of african-american

00:51:44.440 --> 00:51:49.300
leaders Martin Luther King senior had endorsed us and the young was on our team

00:51:49.300 --> 00:51:54.680
great civil rights leaders here in in Atlanta were behind us others that got

00:51:54.680 --> 00:51:58.290
to know us it was a real asset to us

00:52:00.480 --> 00:52:06.790
the liberals that I had worked with got nervous in a room full of black people, and Jimmy Carter

00:52:06.790 --> 00:52:16.350
didn't. He was very comfortable, very relaxed. When I talked with him, I realized that he read

00:52:16.350 --> 00:52:25.710
more. He was more disciplined, more organized. His personal life was more meaningful. His religion

00:52:25.710 --> 00:52:30.000
was really way down deep in the marrow.

00:52:30.000 --> 00:52:37.960
his bones uh and and i i said you know that's the kind of guy that ought to be running this country

00:52:41.130 --> 00:52:47.400
most candidates stayed away from florida confident the little known georgian could be dealt with

00:52:47.400 --> 00:52:55.060
later there really was an underestimation of carter from the beginning in that 76 campaign

00:52:55.060 --> 00:53:00.000
and he took advantage of that repeatedly carter in no way played the southern rude

00:53:00.000 --> 00:53:03.980
but there was a little bit of this sneaking up on everybody.

00:53:03.980 --> 00:53:07.380
By decisively defeating George Wallace,

00:53:07.380 --> 00:53:10.780
he not only succeeded in doing what the liberals

00:53:10.780 --> 00:53:13.440
wanted him to do, but transforming himself

00:53:13.440 --> 00:53:17.380
into a really powerful major candidate.

00:53:17.380 --> 00:53:20.720
What the liberals had not realized

00:53:20.720 --> 00:53:23.280
is that by the time of Florida,

00:53:23.280 --> 00:53:25.770
Jimmy Carter would have won Iowa,

00:53:25.770 --> 00:53:28.170
would have won New Hampshire,

00:53:28.170 --> 00:53:30.000
and would have this huge retinue

00:53:30.000 --> 00:53:36.620
press following him around and he was the man to beat Carter's stamina seemed

00:53:36.620 --> 00:53:43.370
superhuman behind that Huckleberry Finn grin one reporter observed there is a

00:53:43.370 --> 00:53:51.830
perfectionist campaign machine that shuts down only six hours out of 24 state by

00:53:51.830 --> 00:54:00.000
state the delegates kept adding up by the time the Democrats convened in New York in July 19th

00:54:00.000 --> 00:54:11.090
In 1976, Carter had a lock on the nomination.

00:54:11.090 --> 00:54:16.930
The fact that Carter could unite the nation, north and south, and give us a clean shot

00:54:16.930 --> 00:54:21.890
for the presidency, this was a culmination of my dreams.

00:54:21.890 --> 00:54:25.890
My name is Jimmy Carter and I'm running for president.

00:54:30.350 --> 00:54:34.180
And now I've come here to accept your nomination.

00:54:37.990 --> 00:54:40.720
He'd pulled off a miracle.

00:54:41.900 --> 00:54:49.600
In the fall of 1975, he was barely visible as a candidate, below 5% in all the polls.

00:54:49.600 --> 00:54:56.400
And suddenly, six months later, he has the Democratic presidential nomination, and he

00:54:56.400 --> 00:55:00.000
is running 70% in the polls.

00:55:00.000 --> 00:55:02.010
public opinion polls.

00:55:02.010 --> 00:55:05.870
That is a miracle.

00:55:05.870 --> 00:55:10.530
Now, the problem was that he had his vulnerabilities,

00:55:10.530 --> 00:55:19.220
and they showed in the fall.

00:55:19.220 --> 00:55:21.860
- Let's go around.

00:55:21.860 --> 00:55:24.120
- In the summer of 1976,

00:55:24.120 --> 00:55:26.320
with a huge lead in the polls,

00:55:26.320 --> 00:55:29.380
the Democratic candidate could relax.

00:55:31.470 --> 00:55:39.000
The press descended on planes, eager to learn about the peanut farmer who might become president

00:55:39.000 --> 00:55:43.990
and the remote southern town he called home.

00:55:43.990 --> 00:55:48.450
It was thought out and carefully planned by the campaign committee.

00:55:48.450 --> 00:55:51.930
As Jody Powell said, our campaign was not really about issues.

00:55:51.930 --> 00:55:57.730
It was about blue skies, where everybody knew each other, and no pollution.

00:55:57.730 --> 00:56:00.000
That was it all the way.

00:56:00.190 --> 00:56:05.110
into planes you see blue skies you see everybody in town seemed to love Jimmy

00:56:05.110 --> 00:56:11.100
everybody was enthusiastic about him so it was perfect this was who he was this

00:56:11.100 --> 00:56:17.080
is where he came from the people in that town clearly saw him as one of them that

00:56:17.080 --> 00:56:22.510
was a tremendous asset when you're campaigning every little picture in the

00:56:22.510 --> 00:56:26.070
paper every little something is free publicity

00:56:26.070 --> 00:56:30.000
so we were trying to impress the people and trying to let them know

00:56:30.000 --> 00:56:37.690
what the real planes was like we went and took this empty depot and steam cleaned it and we

00:56:37.690 --> 00:56:43.580
brought furniture from our homes and pictures off the walls everybody just cooperated and wanted to

00:56:43.580 --> 00:56:53.280
help back home surrounded by family and friends carter would display his best qualities you would

00:56:53.280 --> 00:56:59.440
go to the church on sunday and there would be jimmy and he'd have the lesson for the day and he'd outline something

00:57:00.000 --> 00:57:02.140
One time he came out and he had underlined,

00:57:02.520 --> 00:57:05.560
the Baptists believe in the separation of church and state.

00:57:05.780 --> 00:57:07.920
So he was safe on church issues.

00:57:08.690 --> 00:57:12.850
You might go out to the pawn house and hear Jimmy Carter come out

00:57:12.850 --> 00:57:15.390
and say what he just heard from a group of experts

00:57:15.390 --> 00:57:20.990
and like an A student in a seminar tell you what everybody said with great clarity.

00:57:23.780 --> 00:57:29.540
And if you asked him a tough question, he got those cold blue eyes.

00:57:30.000 --> 00:57:34.100
And reporters would just shudder with delight in that look.

00:57:34.100 --> 00:57:37.090
And so what you could see, he'd be a tough son of a bitch.

00:57:37.090 --> 00:57:41.150
So not only was he moral, and did he have all these people love him, but he would be

00:57:41.150 --> 00:57:42.150
tough.

00:57:42.150 --> 00:57:49.880
Carter's eccentric family provided color.

00:57:49.880 --> 00:57:54.530
Sister Gloria rode a Harley Davidson and was a born-again Christian.

00:57:54.530 --> 00:58:00.000
Sister Ruth was a charismatic Christian and popular faith healer, and holding forth

00:58:00.000 --> 00:58:02.620
at his filling station across from the depot

00:58:02.620 --> 00:58:06.260
was Billy, Carter's hard-drinking brother.

00:58:07.620 --> 00:58:08.380
I'll take advantage.

00:58:09.900 --> 00:58:11.480
Sam Dawson was against me.

00:58:12.280 --> 00:58:14.770
Well, Brother Billy, he was a sport.

00:58:15.250 --> 00:58:16.430
He was a very good businessman,

00:58:16.830 --> 00:58:18.290
and he was extremely colorful.

00:58:19.080 --> 00:58:20.700
And he was much brighter,

00:58:20.960 --> 00:58:23.140
much more learned and well-read

00:58:23.140 --> 00:58:24.140
than most people think.

00:58:24.140 --> 00:58:27.290
He read a book every day

00:58:27.290 --> 00:58:30.000
and had over 20,000 in his library's

00:58:30.000 --> 00:58:31.990
stashed up in his attic when he died.

00:58:33.610 --> 00:58:35.170
Billy ended up with a reputation,

00:58:35.170 --> 00:58:41.590
and then he tried to live up to it.

00:58:41.590 --> 00:58:45.470
Of all the Carters, it was the irrepressible Miss Lillian

00:58:45.470 --> 00:58:48.510
who best reflected on Jimmy.

00:58:48.510 --> 00:58:53.400
Since her husband's death, she had lived life on her own terms.

00:58:53.400 --> 00:58:56.310
Always committed to helping the poor,

00:58:56.310 --> 00:59:00.000
she had joined the Peace Corps and spent two years in India.

00:59:01.050 --> 00:59:03.810
There's that wonderful story of Ms. Lillian

00:59:03.810 --> 00:59:06.250
when one reporter, a woman from New York,

00:59:06.250 --> 00:59:09.570
came down to Plains, and Ms. Lillian greeted her and said,

00:59:09.570 --> 00:59:11.470
"You know, welcome to Plains.

00:59:11.470 --> 00:59:13.010
You know, it's so nice to see you.

00:59:13.010 --> 00:59:14.630
Would you like some lemonade?

00:59:14.630 --> 00:59:15.830
How was your journey?

00:59:15.830 --> 00:59:17.570
Your dress is beautiful."

00:59:17.570 --> 00:59:19.470
You know, pouring on the Southern hospitality,

00:59:19.470 --> 00:59:22.150
and the reporter jumped right in on Ms. Lillian and said,

00:59:22.150 --> 00:59:24.750
"Now, Ms. Lillian, your son is running for president,

00:59:24.750 --> 00:59:26.110
saying he'll never tell a lie.

00:59:26.110 --> 00:59:29.370
As a mother, are you telling me he's never told a lie?"

00:59:29.370 --> 00:59:30.000
She goes, "Oh, well,

00:59:30.000 --> 00:59:32.450
"Well, Jimmy tells white lies all the time."

00:59:32.450 --> 00:59:34.030
The reporter said, "Well, tell me, what do you mean?

00:59:34.030 --> 00:59:35.410
"What is a white lie?"

00:59:35.410 --> 00:59:37.810
And Ms. Lillian said, "Remember when I said,

00:59:37.810 --> 00:59:40.590
"Welcome to Plains and how good it is to see you?

00:59:40.590 --> 00:59:42.230
"That's the white lie."

00:59:42.230 --> 00:59:44.030
- Well, now, it's sometimes said that the parents

00:59:44.030 --> 00:59:46.770
are never really satisfied with what their children accomplish.

00:59:46.770 --> 00:59:48.870
- I won't be satisfied 'til he gets in the White House.

00:59:48.870 --> 00:59:49.710
- You think he will?

00:59:49.710 --> 00:59:51.350
- I know he will.

00:59:51.350 --> 00:59:52.910
- And then what are you gonna do?

00:59:52.910 --> 00:59:55.320
- I'm gonna stay out at the Pine House and fish.

01:00:00.640 --> 01:00:03.500
ELECTION MEANS A LOT TO OUR COUNTRY.

01:00:03.500 --> 01:00:07.340
CARTER BEGAN THE FALL CAMPAIGN AGAINST INCUMBENT PRESIDENT

01:00:07.340 --> 01:00:10.550
GERALD FORWARD WITH A 15-POINT LEAD.

01:00:10.550 --> 01:00:13.410
WE'VE BEEN DISAPPOINTED, DISILLUSIONED.

01:00:13.410 --> 01:00:15.370
WE'VE BEEN KICKED OUT OF GOVERNMENT.

01:00:15.370 --> 01:00:16.890
WE'VE BEEN EMBARRISHED.

01:00:16.890 --> 01:00:18.330
SOMETIMES WE'VE BEEN ASHAMED.

01:00:18.330 --> 01:00:21.230
HE RETURNED TO THE THEMES OF HONESTY AND TRUST

01:00:21.230 --> 01:00:23.910
THAT HAD DEFINED HIS PRIMARY CAMPAIGN.

01:00:23.910 --> 01:00:27.460
JIMMY CARTER WILL SAY ANYTHING, ANYWHERE,

01:00:27.460 --> 01:00:30.000
TO BE PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES.

01:00:30.000 --> 01:00:39.700
day approached he was pressured to take a stand on the issues he was a moderate to the moderates

01:00:39.700 --> 01:00:46.160
he was a conservative to the conservatives he was a liberal to the liberals and in fact he was all

01:00:46.160 --> 01:00:50.570
of those things we're going to have a fair government once again we're going to have a

01:00:50.570 --> 01:00:56.700
government that's open and not secret once again his standard line when asked about his foreign

01:00:56.700 --> 01:01:00.000
policy was that he wanted to provide a foreign

01:01:00.030 --> 01:01:03.330
policy as good as the American people.

01:01:03.330 --> 01:01:06.330
Well, gee, that's great, but what in the world does it mean?

01:01:06.330 --> 01:01:07.290
You can defend on it.

01:01:07.290 --> 01:01:08.370
You help me.

01:01:08.370 --> 01:01:09.370
I'll help you.

01:01:09.370 --> 01:01:10.210
Thank you very much.

01:01:10.210 --> 01:01:14.610
The gist of what he presented was that he would be a centrist

01:01:14.610 --> 01:01:18.800
Democrat who had liberal values in his heart,

01:01:18.800 --> 01:01:22.080
as well as a desire for frugality and thrift

01:01:22.080 --> 01:01:23.980
and efficiency in government.

01:01:23.980 --> 01:01:27.820
And so he could appeal to people from all parts

01:01:27.820 --> 01:01:30.000
of the Democratic Party.

01:01:31.180 --> 01:01:38.240
But as Julian Bond said at one point, the problem with this is that his support was an inch deep and a mile wide.

01:01:38.240 --> 01:01:49.970
Alarmed that support among liberal Democrats was eroding, Carter's young staff made a bold move.

01:01:49.970 --> 01:01:59.160
We did the Playboy interview to show that being a born-again Christian was not a threat to more secular Democrats and young people.

01:02:01.120 --> 01:02:09.020
for five hours carter tried to explain his views on culture politics and faith toward the end of

01:02:09.020 --> 01:02:16.740
the interview exasperated at not being understood he said i've looked on a lot of women with lust

01:02:16.740 --> 01:02:24.430
i've committed adultery in my heart many times if you read the interview the lust in your heart line

01:02:24.430 --> 01:02:30.000
was to try to explain that he too was a sinner but the language was and i would see this all the time he

01:02:30.000 --> 01:02:32.840
Carter used language that was driven to his world,

01:02:32.840 --> 01:02:35.740
to his, like we all do, to our own cultural context.

01:02:35.740 --> 01:02:39.540
Here's a guy who is so moral, but on the one hand,

01:02:39.540 --> 01:02:43.190
he talks about he's lusted after women in his heart,

01:02:43.190 --> 01:02:46.430
and he talks about shacking up, and he uses language

01:02:46.430 --> 01:02:51.700
that's going to really enrage and turn off a lot of people.

01:02:51.700 --> 01:02:53.940
Do not underestimate what a crisis that interview

01:02:53.940 --> 01:02:56.450
in "The Lust in My Heart" caused Carter

01:02:56.450 --> 01:02:59.180
and almost derailed the entire Carter campaign.

01:02:59.180 --> 01:03:00.000
They were in habit.

01:03:00.000 --> 01:03:06.070
In retrospect it was kind of amusing, it wasn't very funny at the time, trying to explain

01:03:06.070 --> 01:03:10.130
to people that Jimmy Carter was not some child molester, you know what I mean, or pervert.

01:03:10.130 --> 01:03:14.640
The Playboy thing has been of great, very great concern to me, I don't know how to deal

01:03:14.640 --> 01:03:15.640
with it exactly.

01:03:15.640 --> 01:03:21.060
By the time Jimmy Carter and Gerald Ford met in the first of two presidential debates,

01:03:21.060 --> 01:03:23.700
Carter's lead had evaporated.

01:03:23.700 --> 01:03:26.910
The momentum belonged to Ford.

01:03:26.910 --> 01:03:29.480
Two weeks later, he blundered.

01:03:30.160 --> 01:03:38.610
There is no Soviet domination of Eastern Europe, and there never will be under a Ford administration.

01:03:38.610 --> 01:03:42.230
I'm sorry, could I just apologize.

01:03:42.230 --> 01:03:47.070
Did I understand you to say, sir, that the Russians are not using Eastern Europe as their

01:03:47.070 --> 01:03:48.150
own sphere of influence?

01:03:48.150 --> 01:03:52.290
We knew that this was going to hurt, and a lot of people couldn't see how a president

01:03:52.290 --> 01:03:54.170
would say that.

01:03:54.170 --> 01:04:00.000
It gave us about a week, as I recall, to pound away on this, and you could just feel people

01:04:00.800 --> 01:04:02.540
moving on that question.

01:04:02.540 --> 01:04:10.900
So what it did, I think, was, rather than electing us, it stopped our slide.

01:04:10.900 --> 01:04:16.360
By election day, the polls showed a dead heat.

01:04:16.360 --> 01:04:23.360
It was not until 3:00 a.m. that the networks announced the winner by one of the closest

01:04:23.360 --> 01:04:25.040
margins in American history.

01:04:25.040 --> 01:04:30.000
I look back now, and I just -- I'm amazed.

01:04:31.080 --> 01:04:35.750
Going from total anonymity to being President of the United States in less than 12 months

01:04:35.750 --> 01:04:37.790
is unprecedented in American history.

01:04:37.790 --> 01:04:42.300
If it weren't for the country looking for something in '76, Carter could never have

01:04:42.300 --> 01:04:43.300
gotten elected.

01:04:43.300 --> 01:04:44.300
He'd never been allowed out of the box.

01:04:44.300 --> 01:04:52.370
He offered a biography of what we wanted to hear: farmer, Main Street values, planes.

01:04:52.370 --> 01:04:57.060
It was the right message at the right time, and it didn't happen by accident.

01:04:57.060 --> 01:05:00.000
Carter created that message knowing that that's what would be.

01:05:00.000 --> 01:05:00.600
win the day.

01:05:01.340 --> 01:05:13.500
I came all the way through, through 22 months, and I didn't get choked up until I...

01:05:13.500 --> 01:05:30.000
This was not planned.

01:05:30.800 --> 01:05:33.140
It was not scheduled.

01:05:33.140 --> 01:05:40.240
And whether this is Carter's surprise for his inocular, by golly, Bob, how about that?

01:05:40.240 --> 01:05:47.790
The morning of January 20th, 1977, Jimmy Carter surprised the nation.

01:05:47.790 --> 01:05:52.440
I remember I was out there walking and you could hear Walter Cronkite over the loudspeaker

01:05:52.440 --> 01:05:56.250
saying, "President is walking down the street."

01:05:56.250 --> 01:06:00.000
It was a major moment of the Carter presidency, symbolically.

01:06:00.000 --> 01:06:08.150
theater. Here was this tremendous breath of fresh air. He was gonna bring

01:06:08.150 --> 01:06:14.210
something new to Washington, bring new people and new ideas. Our commitment to

01:06:14.210 --> 01:06:24.800
human rights must be absolute. Our laws fair. It was so different from what had come before.

01:06:24.800 --> 01:06:30.000
People were looking for something that was simple, something that was pure.

01:06:30.410 --> 01:06:34.120
And it just struck a chord in the American people.

01:06:34.120 --> 01:06:38.870
More is not necessarily better.

01:06:38.870 --> 01:06:40.970
Jimmy Carter was exactly what the American people

01:06:40.970 --> 01:06:42.790
always say they want.

01:06:42.790 --> 01:06:45.920
Above politics, determined to do the right thing

01:06:45.920 --> 01:06:48.550
regardless of political consequences,

01:06:48.550 --> 01:06:52.820
a simple person who doesn't lie, a modest man,

01:06:52.820 --> 01:06:56.970
not somebody with a lot of imperial pretenses.

01:06:56.970 --> 01:06:58.350
That's what people say they want.

01:06:58.350 --> 01:07:00.000
And that's what they got with Jimmy Carter.

01:07:02.520 --> 01:07:07.920
The Carter team arrived in Washington full of confidence, ready to take on the Washington

01:07:07.920 --> 01:07:09.960
insiders they had run against.

01:07:09.960 --> 01:07:16.330
I felt like the advance wave of the German army arriving in Paris in 1940.

01:07:16.330 --> 01:07:19.540
I mean, this is a democratic city, and they were terrified.

01:07:19.540 --> 01:07:20.620
I mean, terrified.

01:07:20.620 --> 01:07:22.860
You could feel it in the air.

01:07:22.860 --> 01:07:27.080
They did not have a lot in common with the national political party.

01:07:27.080 --> 01:07:30.000
They did not have a lot in common with the Congress.

01:07:30.000 --> 01:07:34.680
close-knit band of brothers, and they were intensely loyal to Jimmy Carter, and they

01:07:34.680 --> 01:07:36.540
were pretty cocky guys as well.

01:07:36.540 --> 01:07:44.910
There was clearly some degree of suspicion and maybe in a little bit of resentment that,

01:07:44.910 --> 01:07:50.280
here come these folks riding in here. They didn't really pay their dues. They're not

01:07:50.280 --> 01:07:55.720
us. They're not our kind of folks. And all of a sudden, they're in the White House, and

01:07:55.720 --> 01:08:00.000
we'll show them that this town is tougher than they think.

01:08:00.000 --> 01:08:03.740
people had no experience in Washington,

01:08:03.740 --> 01:08:07.290
and they were sort of contemptuous of Washington.

01:08:07.290 --> 01:08:09.830
Well, it's one thing to sort of run against Washington,

01:08:09.830 --> 01:08:12.710
but you have to live there, and you have to govern there,

01:08:12.710 --> 01:08:16.600
and you have to work with the people who are there.

01:08:16.600 --> 01:08:20.430
And it really doesn't get you anywhere

01:08:20.430 --> 01:08:23.370
to have this attitude if you want to get anything done.

01:08:23.370 --> 01:08:25.300
- You get things done by power.

01:08:25.300 --> 01:08:28.190
You get power from having public support.

01:08:28.190 --> 01:08:30.000
My argument was in order to maintain power,

01:08:30.280 --> 01:08:32.820
we would have to reinforce constantly

01:08:32.820 --> 01:08:36.140
the message of what we were doing.

01:08:36.140 --> 01:08:37.690
- Good evening.

01:08:37.690 --> 01:08:39.850
Tomorrow will be two weeks since I became president.

01:08:39.850 --> 01:08:43.090
- On February 2nd, Carter addressed the nation

01:08:43.090 --> 01:08:46.560
in a fireside chat on energy.

01:08:46.560 --> 01:08:51.120
The country had been through an oil scare in 1973.

01:08:51.120 --> 01:08:53.000
To head off a new crisis,

01:08:53.000 --> 01:08:55.320
Carter appealed directly to Americans

01:08:55.320 --> 01:08:57.280
to rally around a new program.

01:08:57.280 --> 01:08:59.640
- All of us must learn

01:09:00.030 --> 01:09:05.900
waste less energy. Simply by keeping our thermostats for instance at 65 degrees

01:09:05.900 --> 01:09:11.350
in the daytime and 55 degrees at night, we could save half the current shortage

01:09:11.350 --> 01:09:16.680
of natural gas. If we learn to live thriftily and remember the importance of

01:09:16.680 --> 01:09:25.540
helping our neighbors, then we can find ways to adjust. Carter led by example. He

01:09:25.540 --> 01:09:30.000
curtailed the use of limousines, canceled magazine subscriptions, and

01:09:30.220 --> 01:09:37.400
unplugged television sets, and put the presidential yacht, Sequoia, on the auction block.

01:09:37.400 --> 01:09:43.020
He turned off the air conditioners, and it was so hot in the White House, people would

01:09:43.020 --> 01:09:50.440
come in there, it was unbelievable, it would be a hundred above in there.

01:09:50.440 --> 01:09:57.200
To save on staff overtime, all White House functions would end at midnight.

01:09:57.200 --> 01:10:00.000
No hard liquor would be served.

01:10:00.000 --> 01:10:05.440
Carter is a low church Protestant where it's a sin not to have a hard wooden bench to sit

01:10:05.440 --> 01:10:07.270
on in church.

01:10:07.270 --> 01:10:09.590
And he brought that simplicity to the White House.

01:10:09.590 --> 01:10:14.730
Paul invited down to the White House every other Tuesday.

01:10:14.730 --> 01:10:18.550
We walked into the private dining room on the first floor off the East Room.

01:10:18.550 --> 01:10:24.990
We looked at the table and there were these little fingertip cookies and tip.

01:10:24.990 --> 01:10:28.010
O'Neal looked at me and he says, "What's this?"

01:10:28.010 --> 01:10:30.000
I said, "I guess that's breakfast."

01:10:30.000 --> 01:10:34.740
So the president walked in, you know, walked around the room and shook hands with everybody.

01:10:34.740 --> 01:10:42.350
And O'Neill looked at the president and he said, "Mr. President, you know, we won the

01:10:42.350 --> 01:10:43.350
election."

01:10:43.350 --> 01:10:53.710
Carter presented his agenda to the Speaker of the House, Tip O'Neill.

01:10:53.710 --> 01:11:00.000
Energy was Carter's number one priority, but it was competing with his long list of

01:11:00.000 --> 01:11:05.760
legislation. Bills on hospital cost containment, urban policy, ethics in

01:11:05.760 --> 01:11:12.870
government. There was nothing in the package to grease the wheels of

01:11:12.870 --> 01:11:19.710
government. When Carter struck from his budget 19 multi-million dollar water

01:11:19.710 --> 01:11:24.570
projects that had been approved by President Ford, congressmen were furious.

01:11:24.570 --> 01:11:30.000
It was absolutely right to take it on. These sort of

01:11:30.000 --> 01:11:33.990
boondoggles and unnecessary, really, pork barrel things.

01:11:33.990 --> 01:11:36.490
But he didn't know how to take it on.

01:11:36.490 --> 01:11:38.370
You have to build political capital.

01:11:38.370 --> 01:11:39.970
You have to build alliances.

01:11:39.970 --> 01:11:42.510
You have to make deals.

01:11:42.510 --> 01:11:44.610
- The quid pro quo was not in him.

01:11:44.610 --> 01:11:45.810
If you came to him and said,

01:11:45.810 --> 01:11:48.210
"Look, we can get so-and-so to vote for us,"

01:11:48.210 --> 01:11:51.630
he would turn a deaf ear.

01:11:51.630 --> 01:11:53.890
- He never understood how the system worked,

01:11:53.890 --> 01:11:56.220
Tip O'Neill would later complain.

01:11:56.220 --> 01:11:59.160
And although this was out of character for Jimmy Carter,

01:12:00.000 --> 01:12:03.260
he didn't want to learn about it either.

01:12:03.260 --> 01:12:06.120
- If your job is to find the public good,

01:12:06.120 --> 01:12:08.800
to arrive at what the public good is,

01:12:08.800 --> 01:12:11.360
and then to articulate it,

01:12:11.360 --> 01:12:14.760
and then you become the voice of the people.

01:12:14.760 --> 01:12:16.290
And when you do that,

01:12:16.290 --> 01:12:20.890
it becomes very difficult to compromise.

01:12:20.890 --> 01:12:23.290
- On one occasion when I was talking to President Carter,

01:12:23.290 --> 01:12:25.400
I said, "Mr. President, you know,

01:12:25.400 --> 01:12:27.480
I've had three presidents before you

01:12:27.480 --> 01:12:29.670
and I'll have several after you."

01:12:30.380 --> 01:12:37.260
I'm telling you, from the vantage point of what I see in the legislative process, you

01:12:37.260 --> 01:12:40.260
will be able to do and what you won't be able to do.

01:12:40.260 --> 01:12:48.650
Now you can accept that or not accept it, but Carter's attitude was members of the House

01:12:48.650 --> 01:12:52.720
and Senate are bad guys.

01:12:52.720 --> 01:13:00.000
Carter put O'Neal and the others like him in the same category with the corrupt Georgia

01:13:00.060 --> 01:13:03.020
that he had been fighting for much of his life.

01:13:03.020 --> 01:13:06.700
The same kind of back-scratching, feather-bedding Paul,

01:13:06.700 --> 01:13:08.000
worrying about the next election,

01:13:08.000 --> 01:13:09.820
worrying about their public opinion polls,

01:13:09.820 --> 01:13:12.480
coming in and not doing what was right.

01:13:12.480 --> 01:13:16.030
- Often he wouldn't return phone calls of leading senators.

01:13:16.030 --> 01:13:20.470
There was a kind of an abrasive attitude he had towards them.

01:13:20.470 --> 01:13:22.430
He'd never show them the respect.

01:13:22.430 --> 01:13:25.810
So they all eventually got bitter and turned on him.

01:13:25.810 --> 01:13:30.000
- Even if he had had a personality transplant,

01:13:30.410 --> 01:13:35.270
and he had spent three hours a night playing poker with Tip O'Neill.

01:13:35.270 --> 01:13:36.950
I don't think that would have made the difference.

01:13:36.950 --> 01:13:42.990
I mean, he was faced with an extraordinarily difficult set of circumstances,

01:13:42.990 --> 01:13:47.690
which in part sprang not only from the political situation,

01:13:47.690 --> 01:13:57.870
but from the lack of a connection between his own views and those of his party.

01:13:57.870 --> 01:14:00.000
There will be no new programs

01:14:00.000 --> 01:14:07.000
"unless they are compatible with my goal of having a balanced budget by the end of my first term," he pledged.

01:14:07.000 --> 01:14:16.820
But liberal Democrats, eager to resume the social agenda of Lyndon Johnson's great society, would not back away.

01:14:16.820 --> 01:14:21.870
There had been an eight-year period when there had been no Democratic president.

01:14:21.870 --> 01:14:29.700
There were a lot of pent-up and legitimate desires by constituency groups for more investment in a whole range of programs.

01:14:30.280 --> 01:14:34.480
Although he sympathized with much of it, all of his instincts were to cut budgets,

01:14:34.480 --> 01:14:40.820
reduce the deficit dramatically, but he was always under pressure from the left to have more spending.

01:14:40.820 --> 01:14:47.830
At a breakfast meeting, Carter berated the congressional Democratic leadership

01:14:47.830 --> 01:14:52.750
for adding $61 billion in new programs to his budget.

01:14:52.750 --> 01:14:59.480
"The Democratic Party needs to remove the stigma of unjustified spending," he said.

01:15:01.470 --> 01:15:04.230
"Mr. President," Tip O'Neill reminded Carter,

01:15:04.230 --> 01:15:08.580
"The Democrats are the champions of the poor and the indigent."

01:15:08.580 --> 01:15:12.470
Carter thought the big social programs

01:15:12.470 --> 01:15:16.580
and large amounts of federal spending would bankrupt the country.

01:15:16.580 --> 01:15:20.590
He could see, I think, very clearly the way the world was going,

01:15:20.590 --> 01:15:24.290
and he knew that old era had to be phased out.

01:15:24.290 --> 01:15:27.970
Carter, looking back, was being very long-sighted

01:15:27.970 --> 01:15:30.000
and saying, "You know, we just don't have

01:15:30.000 --> 01:15:37.620
an open-ended never-ending amount of money to spend we have to get things in

01:15:37.620 --> 01:15:44.850
balance Carter's commitment to fiscal restraint appealed to a growing number

01:15:44.850 --> 01:15:51.450
of Americans he brings to the office a refreshing habit of plain words and

01:15:51.450 --> 01:15:57.770
simple manners wrote Newsweek a mind and discipline of temperate steel and an

01:15:57.770 --> 01:15:59.920
insatiable appetite for work

01:16:03.360 --> 01:16:11.130
jimmy carter had entered the presidency with only 51 percent of the vote by june he enjoyed

01:16:11.130 --> 01:16:20.830
an approval rating of over 70 percent then came an event that rocked the foundation of

01:16:20.830 --> 01:16:30.000
the carter presidency it was called the lance affair in july 1977 carter's budget director

01:16:30.060 --> 01:16:33.060
was accused of financial improprieties

01:16:33.060 --> 01:16:36.060
at his bank in Calhoun, Georgia.

01:16:36.060 --> 01:16:41.240
A federal investigation cleared Lance of all illegal activity,

01:16:41.240 --> 01:16:44.130
but concluded that he had engaged in unsafe

01:16:44.130 --> 01:16:47.130
and unsound banking practices.

01:16:47.130 --> 01:16:52.690
Bert Lance is a man of competence and a man of integrity.

01:16:52.690 --> 01:16:55.610
Believing the affair was behind him,

01:16:55.610 --> 01:16:57.500
Carter stood by his friend.

01:16:57.500 --> 01:17:00.000
Bert Lance enjoys my computer.

01:17:00.800 --> 01:17:09.000
confidence and support i'm proud to have him as part of my administration carter had miscalculated

01:17:09.000 --> 01:17:18.560
to the press the issue was ethics not the law sensing a scandal they went on the attack

01:17:18.560 --> 01:17:24.080
there were a lot of journalists who very much wanted

01:17:24.080 --> 01:17:30.000
to prove that they could be as tough on a democratic president as they had been on

01:17:30.000 --> 01:17:32.290
under Richard Nixon.

01:17:32.290 --> 01:17:36.090
There was a real desire to make sure that it was clear

01:17:36.090 --> 01:17:40.200
that they were gonna pursue this every bit as aggressively.

01:17:40.200 --> 01:17:42.580
- One of the things people like to go after

01:17:42.580 --> 01:17:46.100
more than anything else is what they perceive as hypocrisy.

01:17:46.100 --> 01:17:48.300
So that you are judged by the standards

01:17:48.300 --> 01:17:50.830
that you set for yourself.

01:17:50.830 --> 01:17:52.630
And certainly Carter's talking about

01:17:52.630 --> 01:17:55.860
I'll never tell you a lie and emphasizing honesty

01:17:55.860 --> 01:17:58.040
provided an easy opportunity.

01:18:00.060 --> 01:18:04.520
- Carter's inner circle urged him to get rid of Lance,

01:18:04.520 --> 01:18:07.620
but he was torn between loyalty to his friend

01:18:07.620 --> 01:18:10.700
and his own reputation.

01:18:10.700 --> 01:18:14.460
For weeks, he allowed the Lance affair to fester.

01:18:14.460 --> 01:18:16.620
- Do you feel you were drummed out?

01:18:16.620 --> 01:18:19.040
- My statement speaks for itself.

01:18:19.040 --> 01:18:21.200
I have no comment about being drummed out.

01:18:21.200 --> 01:18:24.960
I said in my statement that I had to analyze

01:18:24.960 --> 01:18:26.700
and question what those circumstances were.

01:18:26.700 --> 01:18:30.000
- The day that I resigned, I came home and I was spent.

01:18:30.380 --> 01:18:35.020
lay down on the bed crying about the situation just from the standpoint of just having run

01:18:35.020 --> 01:18:38.340
out of any adrenaline or emotion or anything else.

01:18:38.340 --> 01:18:43.350
And so we had all that horde of media out on the front yard that had been there constantly.

01:18:44.250 --> 01:18:45.690
Yes, it was a suicide watch.

01:18:47.250 --> 01:18:50.520
Looking back, he wasn't that big a deal.

01:18:51.740 --> 01:18:58.930
But what it did do at the time was...

01:19:00.540 --> 01:19:06.040
Give the first blow to the image that Carter was trying to project,

01:19:06.040 --> 01:19:10.510
that his was a squeaky-clean administration.

01:19:10.510 --> 01:19:13.720
Whether my own credibility has been damaged, I can't say.

01:19:13.720 --> 01:19:17.710
I would guess to some degree an unpleasant situation...

01:19:17.710 --> 01:19:21.420
Carter's approval rating plunged 25 points.

01:19:21.420 --> 01:19:24.420
...somewhat, but I just have to...

01:19:24.420 --> 01:19:26.840
It would have been better for the president

01:19:26.840 --> 01:19:30.000
if we had brought that to an end sooner.

01:19:30.920 --> 01:19:33.320
It threw us off our stride.

01:19:33.870 --> 01:19:36.210
It made it harder for us to talk about other things.

01:19:37.200 --> 01:19:39.340
Sort of played into questions about

01:19:39.340 --> 01:19:41.770
whether we could lead and run the country.

01:19:42.700 --> 01:19:45.360
Until that moment, we had been driving the agenda.

01:19:45.700 --> 01:19:47.020
Everyone danced to our tune.

01:19:49.110 --> 01:19:50.990
After that, we danced to everybody else's tune.

01:19:51.500 --> 01:19:52.500
And that hurt us from the public

01:19:52.500 --> 01:19:54.480
because now Jimmy Carter is not in charge.

01:19:58.220 --> 01:20:00.000
Only nine months in office,

01:20:00.250 --> 01:20:08.450
Jimmy Carter was a wounded leader, struggling to regain the confidence of the American people.

01:20:08.450 --> 01:20:22.400
He would succeed where others had failed and face challenges no one could have imagined.

01:20:30.540 --> 01:20:32.780
- From the peanut fields of Georgia

01:20:32.780 --> 01:20:34.800
all the way to the White House,

01:20:34.800 --> 01:20:38.440
Jimmy Carter had accomplished one of the greatest triumphs

01:20:38.440 --> 01:20:41.150
in American political history.

01:20:41.150 --> 01:20:42.810
- He pulled off a miracle.

01:20:42.810 --> 01:20:45.710
In the fall of 1975,

01:20:45.710 --> 01:20:48.240
he was barely visible as a candidate.

01:20:48.240 --> 01:20:49.260
Six months later,

01:20:49.260 --> 01:20:53.080
he has the Democratic presidential nomination.

01:20:53.080 --> 01:20:56.270
Now that is a miracle.

01:20:56.270 --> 01:20:59.640
- He offered a biography of what we wanted to hear.

01:21:00.120 --> 01:21:07.180
farmer, Main Street values, planes, it was the right message at the right time.

01:21:07.180 --> 01:21:10.280
I'll never tell a lie.

01:21:10.280 --> 01:21:18.610
He had promised the nation a new beginning, to heal the wounds of Watergate and Vietnam,

01:21:18.610 --> 01:21:23.490
a government as good and decent and compassionate as the American people.

01:21:23.490 --> 01:21:30.000
What he had was a moral ideology, and the issues where he proved successful

01:21:30.000 --> 01:21:35.880
The Panama Canal treaties, the human rights crusade, peace in the Middle East, those were

01:21:35.880 --> 01:21:38.500
issues where his moral ideology guided him.

01:21:38.500 --> 01:21:44.540
The one argument that I would find would ruin a person's case is when he'd say, "This is

01:21:44.540 --> 01:21:45.540
good for you politically."

01:21:45.540 --> 01:21:47.810
He didn't want to hear that.

01:21:47.810 --> 01:21:50.090
He wanted to know what's right.

01:21:50.090 --> 01:21:56.790
But the man who had pledged to restore honesty and trust to government would find his own

01:21:56.790 --> 01:22:00.000
integrity attacked when his friend and budget chief

01:22:00.000 --> 01:22:05.850
Chief Bert Lance was accused of financial improprieties.

01:22:05.850 --> 01:22:08.290
- Until that moment, we had been driving the agenda.

01:22:08.290 --> 01:22:10.200
Everyone danced to our tune.

01:22:10.200 --> 01:22:12.530
After that, we danced to everybody else's tune.

01:22:12.530 --> 01:22:13.790
And that hurt us from the public

01:22:13.790 --> 01:22:16.770
because now Jimmy Carter's not in charge.

01:22:16.770 --> 01:22:21.310
- He's a very, very smart man and very well-intentioned.

01:22:21.310 --> 01:22:25.240
But feel, feel is very, very important in politics,

01:22:25.240 --> 01:22:26.900
especially in a president.

01:22:26.900 --> 01:22:30.000
And Carter just didn't have very much of it.

01:22:31.280 --> 01:22:38.270
only nine months in office jimmy carter was a president in trouble the economy spinning out of

01:22:38.270 --> 01:22:47.540
control a growing energy crisis his agenda stalled in congress but carter's greatest

01:22:47.540 --> 01:22:56.040
test was yet to come half a world away in iran when 53 americans were taken hostage

01:22:56.570 --> 01:23:00.000
by muslim fundamentalists

01:23:00.760 --> 01:23:09.610
The whole world saw these people stomping on images of Carter, burning American flags.

01:23:09.610 --> 01:23:18.410
The most rancid sort of disrespect and hatred of the United States on television, around

01:23:18.410 --> 01:23:22.050
the world, all the time.

01:23:22.050 --> 01:23:25.170
No one can know how much pressure there was on Jimmy.

01:23:25.170 --> 01:23:27.110
And I would be like, "Why don't you do something?"

01:23:27.110 --> 01:23:28.640
And he said, "What would you want me to do?"

01:23:28.640 --> 01:23:30.000
I said, "Mind the harbors."

01:23:30.000 --> 01:23:31.660
"Okay, suppose I'm on the harvest."

01:23:31.660 --> 01:23:33.960
And they decide to take one hostage out every day

01:23:33.960 --> 01:23:35.430
and kill him.

01:23:35.430 --> 01:23:37.550
What am I gonna do then?

01:23:37.550 --> 01:23:41.620
- To react in a way that was strong and powerful

01:23:41.620 --> 01:23:44.020
would have set us off down a road

01:23:44.020 --> 01:23:48.480
that no man could save where it might lead.

01:23:48.480 --> 01:23:52.760
- Fairly or not, it came to symbolize the question

01:23:52.760 --> 01:23:57.460
of whether Carter was a leader, whether he was competent,

01:23:57.460 --> 01:23:59.250
whether he was strong.

