from viewers like you.

01:25:48.370 --> 01:25:48.980
Thank you.

01:26:15.740 --> 01:26:24.100
22nd 1977 before the graduating class at the university of notre dame president jimmy carter

01:26:24.100 --> 01:26:30.000
unveiled a new foreign policy for the united states being confident

01:26:30.000 --> 01:26:36.640
of our own future, we are now free of that inordinate fear of communism.

01:26:36.640 --> 01:26:42.190
Carter had come to office with no experience in foreign affairs, but determined to make

01:26:42.190 --> 01:26:45.430
his mark.

01:26:45.430 --> 01:26:51.930
In his first year alone, he met more than 40 heads of state, resumed talks on diplomatic

01:26:51.930 --> 01:27:00.000
relations with China, and with the Soviet Union on arms control.

01:27:00.000 --> 01:27:06.280
a new peace process in the Middle East and signed a new canal treaty with Panama

01:27:06.280 --> 01:27:14.490
transferring after 75 years ownership of the canal to the Panamanians but it was

01:27:14.490 --> 01:27:20.140
a principle straight from his heart that would redefine America's role in the

01:27:20.140 --> 01:27:25.790
world we've reaffirmed America's commitment to human rights as a

01:27:25.790 --> 01:27:30.000
fundamental tenant of our foreign policy that was his greatest

01:27:30.000 --> 01:27:36.530
speech standing up for our own values and expecting then that the world would

01:27:36.530 --> 01:27:40.850
appreciate that and that we would be he didn't use this phrase but it's an old

01:27:40.850 --> 01:27:45.920
phrase in American history like the beacon on the hill the beacon of freedom

01:27:45.920 --> 01:27:54.180
and liberty and democracy he will be remembered for putting on the agenda

01:27:54.180 --> 01:28:00.000
hereafter the whole issue of human rights we now assume that

01:28:00.000 --> 01:28:06.760
that the goal of a state is not only to protect its national security interests, it has an

01:28:06.760 --> 01:28:13.940
obligation to try to deal with human suffering where it has the ability to do that.

01:28:13.940 --> 01:28:21.720
I feel very deeply that when people are put in prison without trials and tortured and

01:28:21.720 --> 01:28:27.150
deprived of basic human rights, that the president of the United States ought to have a right

01:28:27.150 --> 01:28:30.000
to express displeasure and to do something about it.

01:28:30.030 --> 01:28:41.210
his idea is that every child is a child of god and based in his faith entitled to the stature

01:28:41.210 --> 01:28:52.380
and respect and the rights of what that means initially human rights was applied aggressively

01:28:52.380 --> 01:29:00.000
to friend and foe carter asked congress to withhold military and economic assistance from

01:29:00.000 --> 01:29:07.420
Latin American dictators in Chile, Argentina, Nicaragua, and decried their human rights

01:29:07.420 --> 01:29:16.750
violations. Cold warriors complained Carter was undermining American allies, paving the

01:29:16.750 --> 01:29:24.020
way for Soviet-backed guerrilla movements to seize power. But they applauded when in

01:29:24.020 --> 01:29:30.000
an open letter to Soviet physicist Andrei Sakharov, Carter promised to seek the release of

01:29:30.000 --> 01:29:34.490
political prisoners held in Soviet jails.

01:29:34.490 --> 01:29:36.930
- The human rights campaign was charging the Soviet Union

01:29:36.930 --> 01:29:38.510
with abuse of its own citizens,

01:29:38.510 --> 01:29:40.310
and they did not like that at all.

01:29:40.310 --> 01:29:43.470
And we now know, now that the Cold War archives are open,

01:29:43.470 --> 01:29:45.550
and from looking at Havel in Czechoslovakia,

01:29:45.550 --> 01:29:47.790
or Lech Walesa in Poland,

01:29:47.790 --> 01:29:49.790
that it was the Carter's human rights policy

01:29:49.790 --> 01:29:52.370
that gave heart to the underground resistance movement.

01:30:02.780 --> 01:30:08.940
But the biggest challenge to Carter's human rights policy would come in the Middle East.

01:30:08.940 --> 01:30:19.670
Iran, because of the great leadership of the Shah, is an island of stability in one of

01:30:19.670 --> 01:30:23.580
the more troubled areas of the world.

01:30:23.580 --> 01:30:30.000
Carter was aware of the brutality of Iran's secret police, and of 2,500 political prisoners

01:30:30.000 --> 01:30:39.720
killed in Iran's jails, but the Shah, installed to the throne in a US-backed coup in 1953,

01:30:39.720 --> 01:30:43.170
had long been a trusted ally.

01:30:43.170 --> 01:30:51.570
New Year's Eve, 1977, in Tehran, Carter reaffirmed America's support.

01:30:51.570 --> 01:30:57.350
Strategic considerations trumped human rights in Iran because the perception of the United

01:30:57.350 --> 01:31:00.000
States was, first, that Iran was a security guard.

01:31:00.030 --> 01:31:04.510
source of oil and it certainly was an important source of oil that it had one

01:31:04.510 --> 01:31:07.450
of the most powerful military establishments in the world which was

01:31:07.450 --> 01:31:11.230
nonsense but the Shah was saying he was going to have the second most powerful

01:31:11.230 --> 01:31:17.230
Navy Iran of course bordered on the on the Soviet Union there are circumstances

01:31:17.230 --> 01:31:22.630
where you have to have a situational morality you cannot go in with the

01:31:22.630 --> 01:31:27.300
notion this is a absolute value we're going to push it all the time I would

01:31:27.300 --> 01:31:30.000
like to offer a toast at this time

01:31:32.460 --> 01:31:41.280
to the great leaders of Iran, the Shah and the Shah Banu,

01:31:41.280 --> 01:31:45.700
and to the people of Iran, and to the world peace

01:31:45.700 --> 01:31:53.990
that we hope together we can help to bring.

01:31:53.990 --> 01:31:58.710
One week after Carter's visit, anti-Shah demonstrations broke out.

01:32:00.000 --> 01:32:06.100
When Iranian secret police fired on the demonstrators and killed several students, religious leaders

01:32:06.100 --> 01:32:09.000
called the Shah's government anti-Islamic.

01:32:09.000 --> 01:32:16.050
- Iran is a very complicated situation, and the Shah was very useful to us.

01:32:16.050 --> 01:32:21.100
At the same time, something else was going on, something very powerful was going on in

01:32:21.100 --> 01:32:22.100
Iran.

01:32:22.100 --> 01:32:25.840
And as I recall, we kind of missed it.

01:32:25.840 --> 01:32:30.000
- We knew there was some resentment, and we knew somewhat of the history of the country

01:32:30.000 --> 01:32:37.060
But we were not conscious, nor were we informed of the intensity of the feelings.

01:32:37.060 --> 01:32:55.940
Since the days of planes and peanuts, the marriage of Rosalind and Jimmy Carter had blossomed into a full partnership.

01:32:55.940 --> 01:32:59.860
Sometime before Carter became president,

01:33:00.000 --> 01:33:04.260
He realized what a valuable advisor to him Rosalind was.

01:33:04.260 --> 01:33:08.260
She was a major player in the campaigns.

01:33:08.260 --> 01:33:12.720
And she did have good rapport with the people.

01:33:12.720 --> 01:33:15.370
She, of course, was ambitious in her own right.

01:33:15.370 --> 01:33:18.430
She wanted to be more than a fashion plate

01:33:18.430 --> 01:33:22.910
and somebody who gave teas.

01:33:22.910 --> 01:33:24.750
The first year Jimmy was in office,

01:33:24.750 --> 01:33:26.470
I became so frustrated.

01:33:26.470 --> 01:33:28.580
Every night, Jimmy would get off the elevator at the White House,

01:33:28.580 --> 01:33:30.000
and I would say, why did you do this?

01:33:30.000 --> 01:33:31.340
Why did you do something?

01:33:31.340 --> 01:33:33.520
And one day he finally said,

01:33:33.520 --> 01:33:34.880
why don't you come to cabinet meetings

01:33:34.880 --> 01:33:37.740
and then you'll know why we do these things.

01:33:37.740 --> 01:33:39.160
So I started going.

01:33:39.160 --> 01:33:42.040
It was always on my calendar and I just listened.

01:33:42.040 --> 01:33:44.160
I didn't participate, but I listened

01:33:44.160 --> 01:33:48.970
and then I knew why the decisions were made.

01:33:48.970 --> 01:33:51.250
- The first child to live in the White House

01:33:51.250 --> 01:33:52.830
since the Kennedy years,

01:33:52.830 --> 01:33:56.890
nine-year-old Amy had the run of the place.

01:33:56.890 --> 01:33:59.380
She roller skated down the marble hallways.

01:34:00.160 --> 01:34:06.080
played in a treehouse her father built for her, and even got a new dog named Gritz.

01:34:06.080 --> 01:34:10.130
She was the apple of her father's eye.

01:34:10.130 --> 01:34:12.950
President Carter hadn't spent a lot of time with his three sons when they were growing

01:34:12.950 --> 01:34:18.930
up, so he tried to put a lot of attention and energy into Amy.

01:34:18.930 --> 01:34:24.950
Keeping with Carter's populist image, Amy was sent to public school.

01:34:24.950 --> 01:34:29.240
The media made much of the fact that her best friend was the daughter of the cook at the

01:34:29.240 --> 01:34:30.000
Chilean embassy.

01:34:30.000 --> 01:34:36.930
She was such a shy, intelligent girl, it was very hard always having that media glare.

01:34:36.930 --> 01:34:49.910
And I think after the White House, she's tried her best to stay out of the limelight.

01:34:49.910 --> 01:34:54.820
One Carter did not shy away from the glare of the media.

01:34:54.820 --> 01:34:58.640
Back in Plains, Billy capitalized on his brother's fame.

01:35:00.000 --> 01:35:06.750
made money on the talk show circuit and marketed his own brand of beer the

01:35:06.750 --> 01:35:12.500
president tolerated his brother's antics he enjoyed the popularity he wrote and

01:35:12.500 --> 01:35:26.480
presented the other side of the Carter family full of fun and laughter in 1978

01:35:26.480 --> 01:35:30.000
the first signs of a gathering economic storm would become

01:35:30.000 --> 01:35:35.940
becoming visible. The stock market was at its lowest point in three years, the trade

01:35:35.940 --> 01:35:39.890
deficit growing, unemployment on the rise.

01:35:39.890 --> 01:35:50.510
The most serious problem that our nation has is inflation, and it's getting worse. It's

01:35:50.510 --> 01:35:59.240
absolutely imperative that Americans commit themselves, all of us, to a common sacrifice,

01:36:00.000 --> 01:36:01.520
rapid increase in prices.

01:36:01.520 --> 01:36:07.490
Carter inherited a no-win economic situation.

01:36:07.490 --> 01:36:13.470
I see him as the last presidential victim of the war in Vietnam.

01:36:13.470 --> 01:36:18.530
Every war this country has fought, once it is over, the economy has to readjust to a

01:36:18.530 --> 01:36:21.690
peacetime economy.

01:36:21.690 --> 01:36:25.600
And what always happens is runaway inflation.

01:36:25.600 --> 01:36:30.000
Carter implored labor and business leaders to keep wages and prices down.

01:36:30.000 --> 01:36:33.000
...and pressured Congress to cut back spending.

01:36:33.000 --> 01:36:35.000
I believe the American people will understand...

01:36:35.000 --> 01:36:37.000
But inflation kept rising,

01:36:37.000 --> 01:36:40.000
his words falling on deaf ears.

01:36:40.000 --> 01:36:44.000
He has this enormous determination

01:36:44.000 --> 01:36:49.360
to go after and do what he thinks ought to be done.

01:36:49.360 --> 01:36:55.180
The capacity to explain, persuade, inspire,

01:36:55.180 --> 01:36:58.190
mobilize, energize the whole country.

01:36:58.190 --> 01:37:00.000
I do not have all the answers.

01:37:00.540 --> 01:37:01.640
Nobody does.

01:37:01.640 --> 01:37:04.540
That was far more tenuous and uncertain.

01:37:04.540 --> 01:37:09.180
But I want to let you know that fighting inflation will be...

01:37:09.180 --> 01:37:13.140
He thought people would just follow, but that didn't happen.

01:37:13.140 --> 01:37:17.270
And I want to arouse our nation to join me in this effort.

01:37:17.270 --> 01:37:21.970
There were growing doubts about Carter's leadership.

01:37:21.970 --> 01:37:27.690
The president, most Americans believed, was too mired in details.

01:37:27.690 --> 01:37:29.640
Was ineffective with Congress.

01:37:30.440 --> 01:37:35.670
had attempted too much and delivered too little.

01:37:35.670 --> 01:37:40.550
This is a classic case where first impressions often sets in with people.

01:37:40.550 --> 01:37:45.110
And the first impressions of that first year were too many things,

01:37:45.110 --> 01:37:49.040
lack of priorities, a lack of accomplishment.

01:37:49.040 --> 01:37:51.280
The fact is, we actually had a good legislative record,

01:37:51.280 --> 01:37:56.260
but we had thrown so much up that in comparison to that,

01:37:56.260 --> 01:37:58.870
the accomplishment seemed to pale.

01:38:00.600 --> 01:38:03.940
I would sometimes say, "Why don't you do this in your second term?"

01:38:03.940 --> 01:38:07.200
And he would say, "What if I don't have a second term?"

01:38:07.200 --> 01:38:09.040
And I think he felt that way the whole time,

01:38:09.040 --> 01:38:12.630
that if something needed to be done, it needed to be done.

01:38:12.630 --> 01:38:18.590
With an approval rating of only 33%, Time magazine concluded

01:38:18.590 --> 01:38:21.820
he has the potential for growing in the office,

01:38:21.820 --> 01:38:25.220
but he does not have a great deal of time left.

01:38:35.150 --> 01:38:37.950
- Nestled in the mountains of Maryland,

01:38:37.950 --> 01:38:42.130
Camp David was Jimmy Carter's refuge.

01:38:42.130 --> 01:38:45.390
It was the place he and Roseland repaired to on weekends

01:38:45.390 --> 01:38:52.010
to get away from the pressures of Washington.

01:38:52.010 --> 01:38:56.330
In September 1978, Jimmy Carter would enshrine

01:38:56.330 --> 01:38:59.190
Camp David and himself in history.

01:39:01.400 --> 01:39:04.540
He had spent a lot of time studying the Middle East.

01:39:04.540 --> 01:39:08.490
He felt very deeply that we should try to find peace over there.

01:39:08.490 --> 01:39:12.650
And boy, he really bet his presidency on that.

01:39:12.650 --> 01:39:14.570
He was already very low in the polls.

01:39:14.570 --> 01:39:17.770
He had practically nowhere to go but up.

01:39:17.770 --> 01:39:21.340
But he still could possibly win a second term.

01:39:21.340 --> 01:39:29.110
And if he failed, that would certainly write him off.

01:39:29.110 --> 01:39:30.000
Since the creation--

01:39:30.000 --> 01:39:33.620
of the state of Israel, every attempt to bring peace

01:39:33.620 --> 01:39:36.530
to the Middle East had failed.

01:39:36.530 --> 01:39:43.240
Refugees, land disputes, terrorism plagued the region.

01:39:43.240 --> 01:39:48.200
Four wars, the last in 1973, had left a bitter legacy

01:39:48.200 --> 01:39:56.810
of hate and mistrust.

01:39:56.810 --> 01:40:00.000
Everyone urged Carter to stay away from what seemed

01:40:00.000 --> 01:40:08.450
intractable situation but he would not be deterred I slowly became hardened and

01:40:08.450 --> 01:40:16.320
as stubborn as at any other time I can remember he wrote the Middle East for

01:40:16.320 --> 01:40:22.290
years had been and was then the place where you thought if we're gonna end up

01:40:22.290 --> 01:40:25.600
blowing up the world that's where it will start if there's going to be a

01:40:25.600 --> 01:40:30.000
nuclear confrontation between the superpowers that's going to come out and

01:40:30.000 --> 01:40:38.350
out of the Middle East. So finding a way to tamp that down was to him extremely important.

01:40:38.350 --> 01:40:45.630
Egyptian President Anwar Sadat took the first step toward peace in November 1977 when he

01:40:45.630 --> 01:41:00.000
became the first Arab leader to set foot on Israeli soil. Carter seized the opportunity.

01:41:00.000 --> 01:41:03.440
welcomed President Sadat and Israeli Prime Minister

01:41:03.440 --> 01:41:07.240
Menachem Begin to Camp David to negotiate a treaty

01:41:07.240 --> 01:41:12.310
that would lay the foundations for peace in the Middle East.

01:41:12.310 --> 01:41:15.150
He brought all of his skills and all the best sides of himself

01:41:15.150 --> 01:41:17.230
to the whole operation.

01:41:17.230 --> 01:41:20.030
And all of the potential in Jimmy Carter

01:41:20.030 --> 01:41:23.450
was out there at Camp David.

01:41:23.450 --> 01:41:27.020
It was really an extraordinary time.

01:41:27.020 --> 01:41:30.000
He had studied things so carefully.

01:41:30.060 --> 01:41:38.380
population of every village in the West Bank and is really hard to think of a

01:41:38.380 --> 01:41:43.790
president in our history who had that much capacity to absorb and retain detail

01:41:43.790 --> 01:41:50.950
he made a mistake at the very beginning he thought and told his aides you know

01:41:50.950 --> 01:41:55.390
we're just going to bring together beg and said dad and they'll talk and they'll

01:41:55.390 --> 01:41:59.150
get to know each other and they'll understand each other well he got them

01:41:59.150 --> 01:42:00.000
there and it turned out

01:42:00.000 --> 01:42:03.360
after the first three meetings they were practically never talking to each other

01:42:03.360 --> 01:42:10.770
and things were going downhill very fast it was mean Carter later recalled they

01:42:10.770 --> 01:42:16.840
were brutal with each other face-to-face discussions became an impossibility on

01:42:16.840 --> 01:42:25.040
day three the expected deadline for an agreement

01:42:25.040 --> 01:42:30.000
Carter had accomplished nothing there must be a way he

01:42:30.000 --> 01:42:35.380
He kept saying, "There must be a way."

01:42:35.380 --> 01:42:40.640
That night at dinner, alone with Rosalyn, he arrived at his solution.

01:42:40.640 --> 01:42:47.770
If the two men could not talk to one another, they would have to talk through him.

01:42:47.770 --> 01:42:53.210
He decided that essentially the Americans would draft the proposal and put the proposal

01:42:53.210 --> 01:42:54.210
on the table.

01:42:54.210 --> 01:42:58.840
You wouldn't have an Egyptian and Israeli proposal, you'd have an American proposal.

01:43:01.020 --> 01:43:05.160
"I must admit that I capitalized on the situation," Carter later wrote.

01:43:05.160 --> 01:43:09.190
"It greatly magnified my own influence."

01:43:09.190 --> 01:43:15.620
Carter devoted himself to drafting an agreement.

01:43:15.620 --> 01:43:21.000
With more than 50 issues to be resolved, the work was painstaking.

01:43:21.000 --> 01:43:29.570
He was remarkably tenacious, persistent, persuasive, tough-minded.

01:43:30.000 --> 01:43:36.190
both with Sadat on some occasions and with Begin on other occasions.

01:43:36.190 --> 01:43:43.220
The people trusted him as an honest broker came to play in a very magical and important way.

01:43:43.220 --> 01:43:47.450
He's seen as somebody who's not a cheat or a liar.

01:43:47.450 --> 01:43:50.790
He is somebody who puts his money where his mouth is.

01:43:50.790 --> 01:43:55.110
My world became the negotiating rooms,

01:43:55.110 --> 01:44:00.000
the study where I pored over my notes and maps of the Middle East, he recalled.

01:44:01.660 --> 01:44:09.980
between sessions i craved intense exercise and lonely places where i could think and sometimes

01:44:09.980 --> 01:44:17.080
pray he had things scheduled after the first week and i was going into washington to do some of the

01:44:17.080 --> 01:44:25.570
things he was supposed to do um and when i would leave to go in they would say don't smile because

01:44:25.570 --> 01:44:29.890
everybody will think it'll be all right don't look grim because they'll think it's failing

01:44:30.440 --> 01:44:32.820
That was hard.

01:44:32.820 --> 01:44:37.240
It was from the depths to the heights all the time at Camp David.

01:44:37.240 --> 01:44:43.220
One minute you would think it was going to pass and everything was so exciting and another

01:44:43.220 --> 01:44:48.680
time it would be just hopeless.

01:44:48.680 --> 01:44:54.250
It always seemed to me that the odds were against success.

01:44:54.250 --> 01:45:00.000
It always seemed like a long shot and so I was, I spent a good bit of my time thinking

01:45:00.000 --> 01:45:08.900
about how are we going to deal with this thing if it collapses shuttling back and forth between

01:45:08.900 --> 01:45:18.820
sadat and bagan carter began to put together an agreement a framework for negotiations in

01:45:18.820 --> 01:45:24.120
the middle east which would address the fate of the palestinians and the future of gaza

01:45:24.120 --> 01:45:30.000
and the west bank and a separate peace treaty between egypt and india

01:45:30.000 --> 01:45:36.800
Israel. Israel would return the Sinai territories occupied since the 1967 War.

01:45:36.800 --> 01:45:48.960
Egypt would recognize the right of Israel to live in peace. On September 14th, day

01:45:48.960 --> 01:45:54.740
10, Carter turned to the issue he knew could derail any progress made so far:

01:45:54.740 --> 01:45:59.030
the dismantling of Israeli settlements in the Sinai.

01:46:00.540 --> 01:46:03.600
Carter was unable to get Begin to make any

01:46:04.460 --> 01:46:07.020
concessions that would really have locked up an agreement

01:46:07.520 --> 01:46:13.330
To the point where Sadat just got fed up and said well, I'm going home. You know, I'm just not going to wait any

01:46:13.630 --> 01:46:17.570
Be here any longer and literally sort of had his coat on and was out the door

01:46:19.840 --> 01:46:23.350
Carter begged Sadat to stay

01:46:23.800 --> 01:46:26.580
appealing to their friendship and mutual trust and

01:46:27.170 --> 01:46:30.000
Reminding him of Egypt's good relations with the United States

01:46:30.000 --> 01:46:42.530
States Sadat decided to remain at Camp David Saturday September 16th Brzezinski

01:46:42.530 --> 01:46:49.170
wrote in his diary the president is driving himself mercilessly he is

01:46:49.170 --> 01:46:52.570
single-handedly written the proposed document for the settlements on the

01:46:52.570 --> 01:47:00.000
Sinai Carter presented the formula to Begin at first he

01:47:00.000 --> 01:47:05.770
He called the demands on Israel excessive: political suicide.

01:47:05.770 --> 01:47:10.500
But in the end he relented, agreeing to submit the question of the Jewish settlements to

01:47:10.500 --> 01:47:11.840
the Israeli parliament.

01:47:11.840 --> 01:47:21.040
Jimmy Carter saw a picture of the three major participants on his desk and he told his secretary

01:47:21.040 --> 01:47:24.430
to find out the names of Begin's grandchildren.

01:47:24.430 --> 01:47:30.000
And so then he wrote little notes, putting in the names of all the grandchildren, he

01:47:30.000 --> 01:47:34.000
to Began and said, "You know, this is not just for us,

01:47:34.000 --> 01:47:36.800
this is for our grandchildren, and let me give this to you."

01:47:37.700 --> 01:47:40.580
And Began was profoundly moved by this.

01:47:47.160 --> 01:47:57.010
The Cab David Accords were hailed as a monumental triumph of diplomacy.

01:48:00.640 --> 01:48:04.280
With his brilliant success and inspired leadership,

01:48:04.280 --> 01:48:10.680
Carter has taken a first big step toward realizing the promise of his presidency

01:48:10.680 --> 01:48:13.290
was the verdict of the press.

01:48:13.290 --> 01:48:18.080
These negotiations provide that Israel may live in peace

01:48:18.080 --> 01:48:21.770
within secure and recognized borders.

01:48:21.770 --> 01:48:25.160
And this great aspiration of Israel

01:48:25.160 --> 01:48:29.540
has been certified without constraint with the greatest degree

01:48:30.000 --> 01:48:34.940
by President Sadat, the leader of one of the greatest nations on earth.

01:48:34.940 --> 01:48:45.800
There will never be a history in the Middle East written without Jimmy Carter's name in the index.

01:48:45.800 --> 01:48:50.910
100 years from now, 200 years from now, people will be talking about the Camp David process

01:48:50.910 --> 01:48:53.910
that began in those Maryland mountains.

01:49:00.280 --> 01:49:09.560
camp david was the plum of his administration this was the crowning glory and it enshrined him

01:49:09.560 --> 01:49:28.560
in history to these two friends of mine the words of jesus blessed

01:49:30.320 --> 01:49:49.830
are the peacemakers, for they shall be the children of God.

01:49:49.830 --> 01:50:00.000
Siddharth and Begin were awarded the Nobel Prize for their contribution to peace. Camp David became

01:50:00.000 --> 01:50:03.660
the touchstone for all future negotiations on the Middle East.

01:50:04.520 --> 01:50:10.270
Yet Carter's great success did nothing to improve his standing with the American people.

01:50:10.900 --> 01:50:18.990
There was something about how we had slipped in the eyes of the American people that prevented

01:50:18.990 --> 01:50:25.670
us from getting what should have been an enormous lift out of this incredible diplomatic feat.

01:50:26.700 --> 01:50:29.390
We thought, boy, this shows we can get things done.

01:50:29.390 --> 01:50:30.000
It does.

01:50:30.220 --> 01:50:31.820
bring peace in a crucial area.

01:50:32.680 --> 01:50:35.060
And there was no movement at all.

01:50:37.520 --> 01:50:38.760
It was very dispiriting.

01:50:49.460 --> 01:50:52.700
At home, President Carter's leadership was in question.

01:50:53.880 --> 01:50:56.780
On the world stage, he kept piling up accomplishments.

01:50:58.340 --> 01:51:00.000
In January 1979,

01:51:00.000 --> 01:51:11.000
In 1979, he received Chinese Vice Premier Deng Xiaoping in Washington to celebrate the establishment of formal relations between the United States and China.

01:51:11.000 --> 01:51:22.900
In June, he met Soviet Premier Leonid Brezhnev in Vienna to sign the Sault II arms control agreement.

01:51:22.900 --> 01:51:30.000
From there, it was on to Tokyo.

01:51:30.090 --> 01:51:31.970
for a major economic summit.

01:51:31.970 --> 01:51:38.520
The country's having this terrible domestic problem.

01:51:38.520 --> 01:51:41.520
The president's somewhere on the other side of the world.

01:51:41.520 --> 01:51:43.680
And not for a couple of days, for weeks.

01:51:43.680 --> 01:51:45.260
And I remember getting on the phone saying,

01:51:45.260 --> 01:51:48.330
"You people gotta come home now."

01:51:48.330 --> 01:51:52.560
In one year, the American economy had spun out of control.

01:51:52.560 --> 01:51:56.310
Gasoline prices had more than doubled.

01:51:56.310 --> 01:51:59.510
Mortgage rates pushed 20%.

01:52:00.860 --> 01:52:03.550
Unemployment kept on rising.

01:52:03.550 --> 01:52:08.080
There are just so many forces at work when inflation becomes rampant.

01:52:08.080 --> 01:52:11.200
Interest rates are high and the business cycle is turning against you.

01:52:11.200 --> 01:52:14.380
It becomes almost impossible.

01:52:14.380 --> 01:52:22.910
Of all the problems facing the nation, most Americans now agreed inflation was the most urgent.

01:52:22.910 --> 01:52:29.220
In the summer of '79, fueled by rising oil prices, it surged to 14%.

01:52:31.280 --> 01:52:34.060
- Inflation makes you doubt the future.

01:52:34.060 --> 01:52:36.180
When you have inflation,

01:52:36.180 --> 01:52:38.480
you don't see as much building going on,

01:52:38.480 --> 01:52:41.560
you don't see as much investment going on,

01:52:41.560 --> 01:52:44.420
you don't see as much hiring going on.

01:52:44.420 --> 01:52:47.210
People weren't seeing their savings growing.

01:52:47.210 --> 01:52:50.790
And as a matter of fact, people were terrified

01:52:50.790 --> 01:52:56.890
that inflation would impoverish them in their old age.

01:52:56.890 --> 01:52:59.380
- Carter acted decisively.

01:53:00.190 --> 01:53:01.890
- To reduce the budget deficit

01:53:01.890 --> 01:53:04.030
and bring inflation under control,

01:53:04.030 --> 01:53:07.250
he cut into social programs.

01:53:07.250 --> 01:53:09.870
New realities, explained the White House,

01:53:09.870 --> 01:53:14.110
must temper our nation's commitment to the poor.

01:53:14.110 --> 01:53:17.110
- It stirred up a hornet's nest of opposition

01:53:17.110 --> 01:53:18.690
from the Ted Kennedy people,

01:53:18.690 --> 01:53:23.590
from the traditional FDR coalition.

01:53:23.590 --> 01:53:27.130
They were very, very angry.

01:53:27.130 --> 01:53:29.830
- African-American leaders felt betrayed.

01:53:30.190 --> 01:53:33.560
and vowed to wage an all-out fight on what they called

01:53:33.560 --> 01:53:39.770
Carter's immoral, unjust, and inequitable budget cuts.

01:53:39.770 --> 01:53:44.510
- The leader who most encapsulated the goals that I wanted

01:53:46.150 --> 01:53:49.150
was Martin King at the end of his life saying to the country,

01:53:49.150 --> 01:53:52.430
we have to do something about poor Americans.

01:53:52.430 --> 01:53:54.910
We're the richest country on the face of the earth

01:53:54.910 --> 01:53:57.990
and we've gotta do something.

01:53:57.990 --> 01:54:00.000
Every time I vote for a Democrat,

01:54:00.090 --> 01:54:07.790
I want that Democrat to have Martin's spirit about poverty in his soul.

01:54:07.790 --> 01:54:11.590
Jimmy Carter ran away from that.

01:54:11.590 --> 01:54:16.910
Across America, frustration was reaching a breaking point.

01:54:16.910 --> 01:54:23.550
In Levittown, Pennsylvania, truckers barricaded expressways to protest high fuel prices, setting

01:54:23.550 --> 01:54:29.060
off riots which left 100 injured and led to more than 170 arrests.

01:54:30.060 --> 01:54:32.940
I thought we were really headed down the tubes.

01:54:32.940 --> 01:54:40.070
I now thought we were in deep, deep trouble, and I thought the president was becoming irrelevant.

01:54:40.070 --> 01:54:45.110
Polls showed Carter falling behind Ted Kennedy as the preferred candidate among Democratic

01:54:45.110 --> 01:54:52.380
voters, and even losing trial heats to the likely Republican nominee, Ronald Reagan.

01:54:52.380 --> 01:54:54.460
I don't think he has control of the situation.

01:54:54.460 --> 01:55:00.000
I think he's a very religious man, a very nice man, but I just don't think he's capable

01:55:00.000 --> 01:55:04.960
There's a lot of people who think he's shaky, you know, and I'm one of them. I think he's kind of shaky.

01:55:05.180 --> 01:55:11.780
I think that he's a really floundering leader. I don't see him as a leader, and I don't look to him for leadership.

01:55:13.360 --> 01:55:21.720
President Carter's approval rating was 25 percent, lower than President Richard Nixon's at the time of Watergate.

01:55:21.880 --> 01:55:28.800
It all seems to be falling down around me in the White House, he told a friend.

01:55:30.480 --> 01:55:38.000
i don't know what to do i think he was losing some of that essential nerve that he has in such

01:55:38.000 --> 01:55:49.320
abundance but just for a brief moment there it was really um i was heart sick and i felt so sorry for

01:55:49.320 --> 01:56:00.000
him carter groped for a way to reassert his leadership one advisor suggested that he give a major speech on

01:56:00.000 --> 01:56:03.840
on energy and put the full blame of the economic crisis

01:56:03.840 --> 01:56:08.090
on the high price of Middle East oil.

01:56:08.090 --> 01:56:13.230
When we drew the Outlands up, he was really quite disgusted.

01:56:13.230 --> 01:56:15.290
This is just more of the same.

01:56:15.290 --> 01:56:17.110
It doesn't address the basic problems.

01:56:17.110 --> 01:56:20.030
People will see this as pablum.

01:56:20.030 --> 01:56:23.260
We need something more.

01:56:23.260 --> 01:56:25.540
Jimmy had made several speeches on energy.

01:56:25.540 --> 01:56:27.280
He was trying to impress upon people

01:56:27.280 --> 01:56:30.000
the fact that they need to conserve

01:56:30.000 --> 01:56:34.280
it just seemed to be going nowhere with the public and so he just said I'm not

01:56:34.280 --> 01:56:38.430
gonna make the speech so he got in a conference call with a senior staff and

01:56:38.430 --> 01:56:43.590
the way he put it very pungently was I just don't want to bullshit the American

01:56:43.590 --> 01:56:54.150
people Archer retreated to Camp David for the next 10 days businessmen labor

01:56:54.150 --> 01:57:00.000
leaders governors pop psychologists and clergy were called to the mountain top

01:57:00.000 --> 01:57:04.170
to participate in one of the most extraordinary episodes

01:57:04.170 --> 01:57:07.990
of presidential soul-searching in American history.

01:57:10.070 --> 01:57:15.400
Basically, this was a kind of self-psychoanalysis

01:57:15.400 --> 01:57:17.780
by Carter and the administration.

01:57:17.780 --> 01:57:20.440
He sat up there and listened to the most

01:57:20.440 --> 01:57:24.750
scalding critiques of his presidency.

01:57:26.700 --> 01:57:30.000
They told me that I seemed bogged down in details, Carter.

01:57:30.060 --> 01:57:36.860
wrote, "That the public acknowledged my intelligence and integrity, but doubted my capacity to

01:57:36.860 --> 01:57:42.660
follow through with a strong enough thrust to succeed."

01:57:42.660 --> 01:57:48.200
Within Carter's own staff, a fierce debate raged over what had gone wrong and what President

01:57:48.200 --> 01:57:52.720
Carter should say to the American people.

01:57:52.720 --> 01:57:57.700
Carter's pollster argued, "The president should address a subject deeper than energy

01:57:57.700 --> 01:57:58.800
or the economy."

01:58:00.510 --> 01:58:05.180
that there was a crisis of the American spirit.

01:58:05.180 --> 01:58:07.910
For the first time, we actually got numbers

01:58:07.910 --> 01:58:11.210
where people no longer believed that the future of America

01:58:11.210 --> 01:58:13.110
was going to be as good as it was now.

01:58:13.110 --> 01:58:14.610
Never in the history of American polling

01:58:14.610 --> 01:58:17.010
had that ever existed, that Americans ever,

01:58:17.010 --> 01:58:20.610
even if they believed it, ever evidenced, would say,

01:58:20.610 --> 01:58:22.570
"Oh, my children are going to have it worse than I am.

01:58:22.570 --> 01:58:23.570
The country's going to get worse.

01:58:23.570 --> 01:58:25.400
We've already had our heyday."

01:58:25.400 --> 01:58:28.910
And that really shook me, because it was so anti-Americans.

01:58:28.910 --> 01:58:30.000
I made the point,

01:58:30.000 --> 01:58:35.380
made the point that if there was a problem with the American spirit it was

01:58:35.380 --> 01:58:40.240
because of the underlying problems of inflation and energy not because there

01:58:40.240 --> 01:58:43.610
was something wrong with the American people I argued that there were real

01:58:43.610 --> 01:58:48.960
problems in America that were not mysterious and were not rooted in some

01:58:48.960 --> 01:58:56.770
kind of national psychosis or breakdown that there were real gas lines there was

01:58:56.770 --> 01:59:00.000
real inflation that people

01:59:00.000 --> 01:59:05.170
People were worried in their real lives about keeping their jobs.

01:59:05.170 --> 01:59:09.420
The vice president of the United States was looking at me and basically accused me of

01:59:09.420 --> 01:59:11.230
being insane.

01:59:11.230 --> 01:59:16.920
So, you had this real division, and then Jimmy Carter ended up by saying, and in this moment,

01:59:16.920 --> 01:59:20.080
I'll never forget it, he ends the thing and saying, he said, I just wanted to hear what

01:59:20.080 --> 01:59:21.080
you all said.

01:59:21.080 --> 01:59:22.740
I've decided I'm going to do everything that Powell said.

01:59:22.740 --> 01:59:25.950
Good evening.

01:59:25.950 --> 01:59:28.130
This is a special night for me.

01:59:28.130 --> 01:59:30.000
On July 15th, after a 10-year-old, he said,

01:59:30.000 --> 01:59:35.600
10-day retreat, Jimmy Carter descended from the mountains of Maryland to deliver the most

01:59:35.600 --> 01:59:37.600
controversial speech of his administration.

01:59:37.600 --> 01:59:43.130
JIMMY CARTER: I want to speak to you first tonight about a subject even more serious

01:59:43.130 --> 01:59:45.830
than energy or inflation.

01:59:45.830 --> 01:59:54.680
I want to talk to you right now about a fundamental threat to American democracy.

01:59:54.680 --> 02:00:00.000
The threat is nearly invisible.

02:00:00.000 --> 02:00:09.920
ways. It is a crisis of confidence. It is a crisis that strikes at the very heart and

02:00:09.920 --> 02:00:11.900
soul and spirit of our national will.

02:00:11.900 --> 02:00:16.840
The speech was more like a sermon than a political speech, and it had the themes of confession,

02:00:17.420 --> 02:00:25.490
redemption, and sacrifice. And he was bringing the American people into this spiritual process

02:00:25.490 --> 02:00:30.000
that he had been through, and presenting them with an opportunity.

02:00:30.000 --> 02:00:37.350
for redemption as well as redeeming himself in a nation that was proud of

02:00:37.350 --> 02:00:46.880
hard work strong families close-knit communities and our faith in God too

02:00:46.880 --> 02:00:54.950
many of us now tend to worship self-indulgence and consumption human

02:00:54.950 --> 02:01:00.000
identity is no longer defined by what one does

02:01:00.000 --> 02:01:04.690
but by what one owns.

02:01:04.690 --> 02:01:08.140
The speech unfairly was labeled the Malay's speech

02:01:08.140 --> 02:01:10.020
because it talked about the fact that the country

02:01:10.020 --> 02:01:12.680
was in a difficult situation, which it was.

02:01:12.680 --> 02:01:16.810
But Americans don't always want their public leaders

02:01:16.810 --> 02:01:19.210
to come to them and say, "Hey, we're in a bunch of trouble."

02:01:19.210 --> 02:01:23.440
When your leadership is demonstrably weaker than it should be,

02:01:23.440 --> 02:01:26.890
you don't then point it to people and say, "It's your problem."

02:01:26.890 --> 02:01:29.350
If you want the people to move,

02:01:30.000 --> 02:01:34.620
You move them the way Roosevelt moved them or you exhort them the way Kennedy or Johnson

02:01:34.620 --> 02:01:35.620
exhorted them.

02:01:35.620 --> 02:01:38.000
You don't say it's your fault.

02:01:38.000 --> 02:01:42.340
The op-ed pieces started spinning out of saying there's nothing wrong with the American people.

02:01:42.340 --> 02:01:43.800
We're great people.

02:01:43.800 --> 02:01:45.860
Maybe the problem's in the White House.

02:01:45.860 --> 02:01:48.560
Maybe we need new leadership to guide us.

02:01:48.560 --> 02:01:51.360
It boomeranged on him.

02:01:51.360 --> 02:01:58.370
If you make a bold stroke like that, you do have to think about how do you follow it up?

02:01:58.370 --> 02:02:00.000
What is day three and day four?

02:02:00.000 --> 02:02:07.450
day five look like? How do you translate that into additional steps? And we botched that.

02:02:09.120 --> 02:02:15.160
To give the impression of a fresh start, Carter asked his entire cabinet to submit their

02:02:15.160 --> 02:02:24.680
resignations. Five were accepted. By firing the cabinet the way he did, Carter just telegraphed

02:02:24.680 --> 02:02:30.000
to the country that he wasn't up for the job. It was a sign of panic. It looked like

02:02:30.730 --> 02:02:33.830
This was a president who was thrashing about, looking for other people to blame.

02:02:37.200 --> 02:02:41.550
Carter's approval rating dropped even lower.

02:02:44.910 --> 02:02:51.190
After all the Camp David meetings, the dramatic speech on July 15th, and the Cabinet firings,

02:02:51.670 --> 02:02:54.570
he is back where he began, one analyst wrote.

02:02:54.570 --> 02:02:59.740
A chief executive rejected by his ultimate constituent.

02:03:00.000 --> 02:03:11.450
The American people that fall the liberal wing of the Democratic Party finally broke with the president

02:03:11.770 --> 02:03:25.150
Throwing its support behind Ted Kennedy the senator from Massachusetts would wage a brutal campaign for the Democratic nomination for Jimmy Carter

02:03:25.820 --> 02:03:30.000
Nothing seemed to be going right. He collapsed

02:03:30.000 --> 02:03:38.140
while running a ten-kilometer race it was taken as a sign of weakness he became

02:03:38.140 --> 02:03:43.180
the butt of jokes when a story broke that he'd been attacked by a giant rabbit

02:03:43.180 --> 02:03:50.990
while fishing in Georgia even the Carter family once thought fun and colorful was

02:03:50.990 --> 02:03:56.390
becoming a liability Billy was investigated for accepting a bribe from

02:03:56.390 --> 02:04:00.000
the Libyan government

02:04:00.640 --> 02:04:10.140
Carter distanced himself from Billy. I have no control over my brother and he has no control over me

02:04:10.420 --> 02:04:18.920
He said on November 4th

02:04:19.940 --> 02:04:25.510
1979 it would all seem trivial a few days earlier

02:04:26.820 --> 02:04:30.000
3500 Iranian students had marched toward the American

02:04:30.000 --> 02:04:38.020
embassy in Tehran threatening to overtake it the anti-sha movement which

02:04:38.020 --> 02:04:45.620
had begun in early 1978 had grown into a full-fledged Islamic revolution the Shah

02:04:45.620 --> 02:04:51.210
was driven into exile and the Ayatollah Khomeini became the leader of a new and

02:04:51.210 --> 02:05:00.000
mysterious Islamic Republic I never had any illusions about Khomeini I didn't have much fun

02:05:00.190 --> 02:05:04.230
with fundamentalist Islam, but I knew he would be a menace.

02:05:05.770 --> 02:05:08.770
If Carter had been more critical of the Shah,

02:05:09.350 --> 02:05:11.690
conceivably it would have been a little more difficult

02:05:11.690 --> 02:05:17.330
for the Ayatollah Khomeini to identify the United States as the great Satan

02:05:17.330 --> 02:05:22.690
and to say everything that is wrong in Iran is basically the fault of the United States.

02:05:23.790 --> 02:05:30.000
Maybe the fact that the United States had been a significant player in Iran since 1945

02:05:30.320 --> 02:05:38.780
was such that it was too late for Carter or anybody to change the deeply hostile nature

02:05:38.780 --> 02:05:42.670
of the Iranian revolution. But it might have made a difference.

02:05:44.800 --> 02:05:49.570
In the first few months of the revolution, Carter had worked to build a relationship

02:05:49.570 --> 02:05:56.340
with the Khomeini regime. But the history of U.S.-Iranian relations would soon catch up with him.

02:05:56.340 --> 02:06:00.000
For months, the deposed shipwriters,

02:06:00.000 --> 02:06:08.480
shah of iran had wandered the middle east then latin america ill with cancer he asked permission

02:06:08.480 --> 02:06:15.920
to come to the united states for medical treatment there were several of us sitting around the table

02:06:15.920 --> 02:06:21.190
talking about whether the president should permit the re-entry of the shah

02:06:21.190 --> 02:06:30.000
we had people tell us that if we let the shah in there could be a real negative repercussion

02:06:30.000 --> 02:06:36.950
in Iran. But the Shah was sort of pathetically flying around the world and

02:06:36.950 --> 02:06:41.690
here's this great country saying well we won't even let you come to one of our

02:06:41.690 --> 02:06:48.680
hospitals. He went around the room and a lot of the people said let him in.

02:06:48.680 --> 02:06:55.530
I argued that he should be allowed in because we treated him as an ally in good

02:06:55.530 --> 02:07:00.000
times and I felt it was our responsibility to treat him as a former

02:07:00.000 --> 02:07:08.770
ally but a friend in bad times i thought american credibility was at stake and he said and if

02:07:10.400 --> 02:07:18.030
then this revolution moves in a way to take our employees in our embassy hostage

02:07:19.320 --> 02:07:30.000
then what would be your advice and the room just fell dead the shah arrived in

02:07:30.000 --> 02:07:36.910
the United States on October 22nd two weeks later the Iranian students seized

02:07:36.910 --> 02:07:44.910
the American Embassy 53 Americans were to be held hostage until the United

02:07:44.910 --> 02:07:53.650
States returned the Shah to Iran everyone awaited words from Khomeini seeing an

02:07:53.650 --> 02:08:00.000
opportunity to consolidate his revolution the Ayatollah gave his blessing calling the US

02:08:00.000 --> 02:08:02.630
embassy a den of spies.

02:08:02.630 --> 02:08:08.930
The United States of America will not yield to international terrorism or to blackmail.

02:08:08.930 --> 02:08:11.650
It was a defining event.

02:08:11.650 --> 02:08:15.890
This is an entire United States government captured and held illegally under international

02:08:15.890 --> 02:08:19.650
law and being taunted every day.

02:08:19.650 --> 02:08:26.230
The whole world saw these images of these people burning American flags, stomping on

02:08:26.230 --> 02:08:30.000
images of Carter, and the most...

02:08:30.320 --> 02:08:37.530
rancid sort of disrespect and hatred of the United States on television around

02:08:37.530 --> 02:08:45.230
the world all the time I would lie awake at night trying to think of steps I

02:08:45.230 --> 02:08:49.890
could take to gain the hostages freedom without sacrificing the honor and

02:08:49.890 --> 02:08:57.930
security of our nation the president wrote Carter rejected all military

02:08:57.930 --> 02:08:59.510
options as too risky

02:09:00.380 --> 02:09:07.060
the problem he said is that we could feel good for a few hours until we found that they had killed

02:09:07.060 --> 02:09:16.150
our people he was determined to bring every one of those men back alive you see the moralism of

02:09:16.150 --> 02:09:21.910
carter the christianity affecting his foreign policy making his belief in each human life

02:09:21.910 --> 02:09:29.640
having a great sanctity to it is not wanting to have blood on his hands a successful statesman have to

02:09:30.160 --> 02:09:39.560
balance risks and sometimes sometimes a risk to a relatively limited number of

02:09:39.560 --> 02:09:50.600
lives down the road saves many many more lives to react in a way that was strong

02:09:50.600 --> 02:09:56.340
and powerful would have set us off down a road that no man could say where it

02:09:56.340 --> 02:10:00.000
might lead people have a hard

02:10:00.090 --> 02:10:09.630
time remembering that this was before the Cold War was over and the the

02:10:09.630 --> 02:10:14.290
possibility of a superpower confrontation in and about Iran had

02:10:14.290 --> 02:10:22.810
always been there and now under these circumstances it was much higher the

02:10:22.810 --> 02:10:27.260
dangers of the Cold War were driven home when the Soviet Union invaded

02:10:27.260 --> 02:10:30.000
Afghanistan Christmas Day

02:10:30.060 --> 02:10:38.860
Three years earlier at Notre Dame, Carter had declared the United States free of that

02:10:38.860 --> 02:10:42.420
inordinate fear of communism.

02:10:42.420 --> 02:10:46.330
But Soviet-American relations had soured.

02:10:46.330 --> 02:10:48.410
Afghanistan was the final blow.

02:10:48.410 --> 02:10:52.920
Have you changed your perception of the Russians in the time that you've been here?

02:10:52.920 --> 02:10:57.270
You started out, it seemed to a great many people, believing that if you expressed your

02:10:57.270 --> 02:10:59.800
goodwill and demonstrated it, that they would reciprocate.

02:11:00.800 --> 02:11:08.260
My opinion of the Russians has changed most drastically the last week,

02:11:08.260 --> 02:11:13.890
than even the previous two and a half years before that.

02:11:13.890 --> 02:11:22.510
It's only now dawning upon the world the magnitude of the action

02:11:22.510 --> 02:11:26.110
that the Soviets undertook in invading Afghanistan.

02:11:26.110 --> 02:11:30.000
I think he had learned that moral affirmation by itself

02:11:30.000 --> 02:11:33.970
didn't necessarily get very far.

02:11:33.970 --> 02:11:38.680
I think he felt that events had built up

02:11:38.680 --> 02:11:43.640
and conspired against him as they had,

02:11:43.640 --> 02:11:48.470
and that the Soviet Union was indeed a real threat.

02:11:48.470 --> 02:11:49.830
- I've cut Soviet access

02:11:49.830 --> 02:11:54.420
to high technology equipment and to agricultural products.

02:11:54.420 --> 02:11:57.430
- Carter leveled sanctions against the Soviets,

02:11:57.430 --> 02:12:00.000
boycotted the Summer Olympics in Moscow,

02:12:00.090 --> 02:12:04.200
and withdrew his sought-to treaty from the Senate floor.

02:12:04.200 --> 02:12:07.950
Encouraged by Carter's new toughness,

02:12:07.950 --> 02:12:10.850
cold warriors who had organized into the Coalition

02:12:10.850 --> 02:12:16.490
for a Democratic Majority arranged a meeting at the White House.

02:12:16.490 --> 02:12:23.310
There was a quite high-powered delegation of the leaders of CDM,

02:12:23.310 --> 02:12:25.300
who at that point included Gene Kirkpatrick,

02:12:25.300 --> 02:12:28.630
who was still a loyal Democrat.

02:12:28.630 --> 02:12:30.000
People went into the meeting,

02:12:30.000 --> 02:12:33.440
thinking he'd say there's a threat to world peace

02:12:33.440 --> 02:12:35.800
that requires an American response.

02:12:35.800 --> 02:12:37.480
And I know you're the kind of people

02:12:37.480 --> 02:12:38.680
who will give me your support

02:12:38.680 --> 02:12:39.980
because you've been saying this

02:12:39.980 --> 02:12:42.000
about the Soviet Union all along.

02:12:42.000 --> 02:12:47.410
Instead, Carter came in and sort of gave the group a lecture.

02:12:47.410 --> 02:12:49.680
He said that he understood that this group

02:12:49.680 --> 02:12:51.960
was interested in human rights, and that was great,

02:12:51.960 --> 02:12:54.320
and he supported human rights.

02:12:54.320 --> 02:12:57.470
We were all terribly disillusioned,

02:12:57.470 --> 02:13:00.000
and I think that almost all of our groups

02:13:00.480 --> 02:13:05.460
either publicly or in the privacy of the voting booth decided we weren't going to vote for

02:13:05.460 --> 02:13:16.580
Reagan. Iran crisis. America held hostage. Good evening. The 100th day of captivity for

02:13:16.580 --> 02:13:24.600
50 Americans in terror. The 100th flag for 100 days of captivity. As spring 1980 approached,

02:13:24.600 --> 02:13:30.000
the hostages had grown into a national obsession. Their memory

02:13:30.000 --> 02:13:34.980
kept alive by millions of yellow ribbons and the nation shares their

02:13:34.980 --> 02:13:42.110
ardeen no stone was left unturned trying to bring them home while Secretary of

02:13:42.110 --> 02:13:48.220
State Cyrus Vance dealt with Iranian government officials Hamilton Jordan met

02:13:48.220 --> 02:13:58.320
secretly with anyone who held out hope our lives became a seesaw of emotions as

02:13:58.320 --> 02:14:00.000
scheme after scheme fell apart

02:14:00.030 --> 02:14:02.150
part, Rosalind later recalled.

02:14:06.130 --> 02:14:10.820
Every time we saw the hostages on television, I counted them.

02:14:11.590 --> 02:14:13.920
Excuse me, what's this book about?

02:14:14.810 --> 02:14:18.790
No one can know how much pressure there was on Jimmy to do something about that.

02:14:19.150 --> 02:14:20.950
And I would go, why don't you do something?

02:14:21.070 --> 02:14:22.710
And he said, what would you want me to do?

02:14:22.770 --> 02:14:23.670
I said, mind the harbors.

02:14:23.970 --> 02:14:25.510
He said, OK, suppose I mind the harbors.

02:14:25.870 --> 02:14:28.530
And they decide to take one hostage out every day and kill him.

02:14:29.100 --> 02:14:30.000
What am I going to do then?

02:14:30.000 --> 02:14:42.580
Fairly or not, it came to symbolize the question of whether Carter was a leader, whether he

02:14:42.580 --> 02:14:50.020
was competent, whether he was strong.

02:14:50.020 --> 02:14:56.880
By April, pressure was growing intense and the situation increasingly hopeless.

02:14:56.880 --> 02:15:00.000
We could no longer afford to depend on diplomacy.

02:15:00.060 --> 02:15:03.170
Carter was forced to conclude.

02:15:03.170 --> 02:15:05.410
I knew from an intelligence report

02:15:05.410 --> 02:15:08.370
that there was little prospect of the hostage's release

02:15:08.370 --> 02:15:12.010
for the next five or six months.

02:15:12.010 --> 02:15:16.830
I decided to act.

02:15:16.830 --> 02:15:20.410
It was called Desert One.

02:15:20.410 --> 02:15:24.520
It required six C-130 transport planes,

02:15:24.520 --> 02:15:29.540
a 90-man rescue team, two C-141 star lifters,

02:15:30.350 --> 02:15:37.150
eight helicopters, and nearly impossible logistics.

02:15:37.150 --> 02:15:39.550
- It was a highly risky operation.

02:15:39.550 --> 02:15:42.250
The CIA even talked about the number of people,

02:15:42.250 --> 02:15:44.550
including the hostages, might be killed.

02:15:45.780 --> 02:15:51.520
But it was doing something.

02:15:51.520 --> 02:15:56.440
- South of Tehran, in the Iranian desert,

02:15:56.440 --> 02:15:59.160
the rescue mission turned into a disaster.

02:16:01.080 --> 02:16:02.460
Two helicopters failed.

02:16:03.790 --> 02:16:06.730
Another crashed into a C-130 in a sandstorm.

02:16:09.980 --> 02:16:11.880
Eight men died in Desert One.

02:16:13.330 --> 02:16:15.070
Three more were severely burned.

02:16:15.890 --> 02:16:19.350
It was my decision to attempt the rescue operation.

02:16:20.600 --> 02:16:22.340
It was my decision to cancel it.

02:16:23.060 --> 02:16:26.790
When problems developed, the responsibility is fully my own.

02:16:28.300 --> 02:16:30.000
I sort of thought at the time,

02:16:30.000 --> 02:16:39.000
well people will give the president credit for trying but I also realized

02:16:39.000 --> 02:16:44.120
that now the chances of being able to get those people out anytime in the near

02:16:44.120 --> 02:16:49.290
future was very very slim and that from a political standpoint that was going to

02:16:49.290 --> 02:17:00.000
be going to be a heavy burden to bear in August 1980 President Carter survived

02:17:00.000 --> 02:17:06.580
the challenge from senator Edward Kennedy for the Democratic nomination the

02:17:06.580 --> 02:17:12.780
campaign had been bitter and divisive but in the end Kennedy had seemed too

02:17:12.780 --> 02:17:23.360
liberal and too tainted by scandal the attack from the left was extremely

02:17:23.360 --> 02:17:29.250
debilitating and the fact that we had a divided party going into the general

02:17:29.250 --> 02:17:30.000
election in nineteen

02:17:30.000 --> 02:17:33.560
against Ronald Reagan was an additional albatross

02:17:33.560 --> 02:17:37.900
beyond the hostage crisis and beyond inflation.

02:17:37.900 --> 02:17:40.740
- Jimmy Carter's administration tells us

02:17:40.740 --> 02:17:43.860
that the descendants of those who sacrificed

02:17:43.860 --> 02:17:46.810
to start again in this land of freedom

02:17:46.810 --> 02:17:50.690
may have to abandon the dream that drew their ancestors

02:17:50.690 --> 02:17:53.170
to a new life in a new land.

02:17:53.170 --> 02:17:55.510
- Republican candidate Ronald Reagan

02:17:55.510 --> 02:17:57.960
launched his campaign on Labor Day

02:17:57.960 --> 02:18:00.000
with a broadside attack delivered

02:18:00.000 --> 02:18:02.900
before an audience of working-class Americans.

02:18:02.900 --> 02:18:05.600
- It is a litany of despair, of broken promises,

02:18:05.600 --> 02:18:08.910
of sacred tusks abandoned and forgotten.

02:18:08.910 --> 02:18:13.380
- Reagan was a very formidable fellow.

02:18:13.380 --> 02:18:16.480
The combination of his beliefs,

02:18:16.480 --> 02:18:18.940
which were not numerous, but they were clear,

02:18:20.860 --> 02:18:23.510
and his acting skills,

02:18:23.510 --> 02:18:27.920
really made people sit up and say,

02:18:27.920 --> 02:18:29.570
"This is, this guy,

02:18:30.320 --> 02:18:35.580
means and believes what he's saying.

02:18:35.580 --> 02:18:39.190
Carter trailed Reagan by more than 20 points.

02:18:39.190 --> 02:18:43.450
With the Soviets in Afghanistan, the hostages in Iran,

02:18:43.450 --> 02:18:48.460
and the economy in shambles, he was vulnerable.

02:18:48.460 --> 02:18:51.810
We were still going through tough economic circumstances.

02:18:51.810 --> 02:18:53.190
People were hurting.

02:18:53.190 --> 02:18:56.080
Interest rates were higher, unemployment was higher,

02:18:56.080 --> 02:18:57.660
inflation was greater.

02:18:57.660 --> 02:19:00.000
When you come right down to it, what kind of

02:19:00.000 --> 02:19:01.920
person should occupy the Oval Office.

02:19:02.440 --> 02:19:08.380
Unable to run on his record, Carter went on the attack, portraying Reagan as a trigger-happy

02:19:08.380 --> 02:19:11.140
cowboy with his finger on the nuclear button.

02:19:14.280 --> 02:19:17.520
We didn't have any cards to play because there wasn't any cards to play.

02:19:18.130 --> 02:19:21.670
We were now trapped by events, trapped by government that couldn't come up with any ideas.

02:19:22.480 --> 02:19:25.380
And basically, we were frustrating the hell out of people.

02:19:25.460 --> 02:19:28.640
But they still trusted Jimmy Carter and Elton to blow the world up.

02:19:30.000 --> 02:19:37.920
was our only hope. On October 28, 100 million viewers, the largest audience ever to watch a

02:19:37.920 --> 02:19:43.960
presidential debate, tuned in. The candidates were running neck and neck. Then the insurance

02:19:43.960 --> 02:19:48.140
would help pay for it. These are the kind of elements of a national health insurance important

02:19:48.140 --> 02:19:53.800
to the American people. Governor Reagan, again, typically is against such a proposal. Governor,

02:19:55.260 --> 02:20:00.000
there you go again. When I opposed Medicaid,

02:20:00.000 --> 02:20:03.360
He had won it in the first half hour by not being crazy.

02:20:03.360 --> 02:20:05.300
He was seeing the same problem before the Congress.

02:20:05.300 --> 02:20:07.140
What had happened was, you could see the shift.

02:20:07.140 --> 02:20:09.540
In the beginning of the debate, over 90 minutes

02:20:09.540 --> 02:20:12.600
was a sense of, he's not dangerous.

02:20:12.600 --> 02:20:13.690
And that's all he had to do.

02:20:13.690 --> 02:20:20.440
The duel of the hostages lengthens to one whole year.

02:20:20.440 --> 02:20:23.210
The Monday before election day played

02:20:23.210 --> 02:20:25.350
like a nightmare for Jimmy Carter.

02:20:25.350 --> 02:20:26.800
Massive humiliation.

02:20:26.800 --> 02:20:28.600
Today, the modulus spelled out conditions

02:20:28.600 --> 02:20:30.000
for release of the American Union.

02:20:30.030 --> 02:20:33.690
American hostages now ending their first year in captivity.

02:20:33.690 --> 02:20:36.350
And the four conditions are the same as those set down in September...

02:20:36.350 --> 02:20:41.210
A good portion of that weekend leading up to it, and all day Monday, Americans were

02:20:41.210 --> 02:20:51.540
literally having their nose rubbed in this embarrassing, irritating, humiliating situation.

02:20:51.540 --> 02:21:00.000
Carter campaigned all day and into the night in Mississippi, Oregon, Washington.

02:21:00.700 --> 02:21:07.590
He arrived in Seattle at 3:00 a.m., the last rally, the last speech.

02:21:07.590 --> 02:21:12.960
I had stayed on the plane to finish up something before I could get off the phone rang,

02:21:12.960 --> 02:21:17.480
and it was Hamilton, Dearden, and Pat Cadell back in Washington.

02:21:17.480 --> 02:21:22.370
They had seen the tracking polls from that day, and they said, "It's basically over."

02:21:22.370 --> 02:21:29.320
The people must decide this election for your sake.

02:21:30.000 --> 02:21:38.180
For the sake of your children, vote, vote for yourselves.

02:21:38.180 --> 02:21:40.540
Tomorrow vote for yourselves.

02:21:40.540 --> 02:21:41.460
Vote Democratic.

02:21:41.460 --> 02:21:42.430
Help us.

02:21:42.430 --> 02:21:43.610
God be with you.

02:21:43.610 --> 02:21:50.160
Went in to hear his speech thinking that I was the only person there who knew that basically

02:21:50.160 --> 02:21:57.420
the election was over and that we had lost.

02:21:57.420 --> 02:22:00.000
It was a landslide.

02:22:00.000 --> 02:22:07.470
won only six states. For the first time in 28 years, the Democratic Party lost

02:22:07.470 --> 02:22:19.510
control of the Senate. On the last day of his presidency, Jimmy Carter stayed up

02:22:19.510 --> 02:22:25.030
through the night. A deal with Iran had been reached. The

02:22:25.030 --> 02:22:30.000
release of the hostages was imminent. A crew from ABC News

02:22:30.000 --> 02:22:46.320
stood by to record the historic moment he wanted to get these hostages home on his watch and this

02:22:46.320 --> 02:22:53.260
was not about getting reelected anymore this was about getting this done because he felt so deeply

02:22:53.260 --> 02:22:59.600
about it we were in the oval office around

02:23:00.350 --> 02:23:06.720
maybe 2:00 in the morning, and nothing happening.

02:23:06.720 --> 02:23:19.880
Dead silence.

02:23:19.880 --> 02:23:22.880
We got to the time where it was 9:00 in the morning.

02:23:22.880 --> 02:23:25.280
We had to be at the inaugural.

02:23:25.280 --> 02:23:30.000
The new president was coming in at 11:00, and finally,

02:23:30.000 --> 02:23:32.380
we all started running off

02:23:32.380 --> 02:23:34.860
and we still had one officer

02:23:34.860 --> 02:23:36.320
back there with a phone

02:23:36.320 --> 02:23:39.220
the hotline in case there was

02:23:39.220 --> 02:23:40.420
any news

02:23:40.420 --> 02:23:44.160
is there any word about the hostages

02:23:44.160 --> 02:23:45.120
have they taken off

02:23:45.120 --> 02:23:48.530
and he was in

02:23:48.530 --> 02:23:50.190
contact with Carter

02:23:50.190 --> 02:23:52.530
all the way up the inaugural route

02:23:52.530 --> 02:23:54.280
and on the platform

02:23:54.280 --> 02:23:57.200
so if there was anything

02:23:57.200 --> 02:23:59.090
that was positive or negative

02:23:59.090 --> 02:24:00.000
he'd hear about it

02:24:03.290 --> 02:24:30.000
And of course the story was that Khomeini released him the minute after Reagan was president.

02:24:30.030 --> 02:24:37.930
1981, 3,000 people gathered at the old train depot in Plains to welcome the Carters home.

02:24:37.930 --> 02:24:49.310
There was a sea of umbrellas out there in the public, standing in the cold and the rain waiting for him, and it was a bittersweet day.

02:24:49.310 --> 02:24:55.920
I think they reacted just like anybody else would have just been rejected by 200 million people.

02:24:55.920 --> 02:24:59.800
It was one of the toughest times they've ever been through, I think.

02:25:02.940 --> 02:25:07.110
He really was better than I was when we came home

02:25:07.110 --> 02:25:09.730
because I was so depressed about it

02:25:09.730 --> 02:25:15.040
that he was always trying to prop me up.

02:25:15.040 --> 02:25:18.730
- The Carters faced trying times.

02:25:18.730 --> 02:25:21.520
Not only had their dreams been shattered,

02:25:21.520 --> 02:25:24.760
but the business they had spent a lifetime to build

02:25:24.760 --> 02:25:28.970
was more than a million dollars in debt.

02:25:28.970 --> 02:25:30.000
- There were some hard days

02:25:30.000 --> 02:25:37.560
that followed they were withdrawn they just wanted to go home and rest and make

02:25:37.560 --> 02:25:46.940
things better and they needed a healing time Carter only 56 was already labeled

02:25:46.940 --> 02:25:54.380
a has-been a shooting star with not even a tail left to fizzle the things that

02:25:54.380 --> 02:26:00.000
they had once loved about him his piety and his simplicity and his his kind of moral good

02:26:00.000 --> 02:26:06.750
goodness they now despised as weakness and moral superiority.

02:26:06.750 --> 02:26:10.920
They just couldn't stand him.

02:26:10.920 --> 02:26:13.380
It wasn't just that he was unpopular.

02:26:13.380 --> 02:26:15.290
People avoided him.

02:26:15.290 --> 02:26:19.600
This is hard to say and hard to believe today.

02:26:19.600 --> 02:26:23.210
People didn't want to associate with him.

02:26:23.210 --> 02:26:29.510
It seemed astounding, Rosalind observed, that after years of important events and decisions,

02:26:30.220 --> 02:26:32.920
The most important thing could be whether the brick walk

02:26:33.020 --> 02:26:35.830
we were building from our house to the street

02:26:36.250 --> 02:26:38.070
was crooked or straight.

02:26:40.680 --> 02:26:44.000
Dad had a woodworking shop and spent quite a lot of hours

02:26:44.100 --> 02:26:46.860
out there working with a piece of lumber.

02:26:46.960 --> 02:26:49.390
He could make a piece of lumber sing.

02:26:49.490 --> 02:26:51.730
And a lot of it's just because of the meticulous care

02:26:51.830 --> 02:26:54.120
that he puts into everything he's done.

02:26:55.620 --> 02:26:58.550
If there was ever an individual who the maxim

02:26:58.650 --> 02:27:00.000
of an idle mind is a dream,

02:27:00.000 --> 02:27:05.280
devil's workshop Carter would perfectly exemplify that he's one of these people

02:27:05.280 --> 02:27:11.620
who simply never rest and never has I think so the question question was not

02:27:11.620 --> 02:27:16.180
whether he was going to do something after he left the the White House the

02:27:16.180 --> 02:27:21.800
question was what he was going to do the Carter's settled in to write their

02:27:21.800 --> 02:27:27.540
memoirs and to make plans to build a presidential library but Carter had

02:27:27.540 --> 02:27:30.000
little enthusiasm for building what he called

02:27:30.000 --> 02:27:35.020
called "A Monument to Myself."

02:27:35.020 --> 02:27:38.240
One night I woke up and Jimmy was sitting straight up in the bed.

02:27:38.240 --> 02:27:40.790
This is after we'd been home about a year.

02:27:40.790 --> 02:27:42.430
And I said, "What's the matter? I thought he was sick

02:27:42.430 --> 02:27:45.820
because he always sleeps all night, even in the White House.

02:27:45.820 --> 02:27:48.550
He can turn things off and go to sleep."

02:27:48.550 --> 02:27:51.680
And he said, "I know what we can do at the library.

02:27:51.680 --> 02:27:54.920
We can have a place to resolve conflicts."

02:27:54.920 --> 02:27:56.760
And so that was the germ of the idea

02:27:56.760 --> 02:28:00.000
for what became the Carter Center.

02:28:00.220 --> 02:28:05.580
by his success in the Camp David Accords, Carter envisioned a place where he could

02:28:05.580 --> 02:28:12.760
host world leaders and mediate civil wars and political disputes. At a cost of

02:28:12.760 --> 02:28:19.310
$28 million, the Carter Center would span 35 acres, include an arboretum, a

02:28:19.310 --> 02:28:23.160
lake, and room enough for a staff of more than a hundred.

02:28:23.160 --> 02:28:30.000
When he set up the Carter Center, he shared with me his vision and I thought,

02:28:30.570 --> 02:28:36.280
that's you know that's that's so grandiose I was frankly I was embarrassed

02:28:36.280 --> 02:28:45.030
for him he was at the nadir of popularity Jimmy Carter was told that it

02:28:45.030 --> 02:28:49.210
would be impossible for him to enter the Naval Academy he was told that it would

02:28:49.210 --> 02:28:53.950
be impossible for him to get elected governor and when he announced for the

02:28:53.950 --> 02:29:00.000
presidency even the Atlanta Constitution had a headline saying Jimmy is running for what

02:29:01.050 --> 02:29:04.950
So all of his life, he had done the impossible.

02:29:04.950 --> 02:29:07.770
And this was just another challenge.

02:29:07.770 --> 02:29:09.830
- Now lift it up, don't scoot it out.

02:29:09.830 --> 02:29:11.610
- Carter's political resurrection

02:29:11.610 --> 02:29:14.780
began unexpectedly with a quiet act,

02:29:14.780 --> 02:29:19.190
just a few miles from Plains, building houses for the poor

02:29:19.190 --> 02:29:24.960
through an organization called Habitat for Humanity.

02:29:24.960 --> 02:29:28.460
- Carter became surprised at the success of Habitat.

02:29:28.460 --> 02:29:30.000
He loves to build.

02:29:30.000 --> 02:29:34.320
carpenter's his hobby so it fit naturally to his own inclination and then

02:29:34.320 --> 02:29:44.460
he started getting great press. In the summer of 1984 when he and Rosalind led

02:29:44.460 --> 02:29:49.020
a busload of Georgians to New York City to rebuild a tenement on the Lower East

02:29:49.020 --> 02:29:52.450
Side it was front-page news in the New York Times.

02:29:52.450 --> 02:30:00.000
It was in stark contrast to kind of the so-called greed decades of the 80s to see

02:30:00.000 --> 02:30:03.400
see somebody not looking to make big speaking fees,

02:30:03.400 --> 02:30:05.750
not looking to sit on corporate boards.

02:30:05.750 --> 02:30:07.690
It's just something about an ex-president

02:30:07.690 --> 02:30:10.290
being so humble in blue jeans with a hammer,

02:30:10.290 --> 02:30:13.770
sleeping on cots and building houses for the poor.

02:30:13.770 --> 02:30:17.400
It's an image seared on our imaginations.

02:30:17.400 --> 02:30:20.220
- When you're there as a private citizen,

02:30:20.220 --> 02:30:24.580
and when you're there because of your faith,

02:30:24.580 --> 02:30:29.730
there's something nice and fresh and humble about that.

02:30:30.000 --> 02:30:31.780
And it's a language people understand.

02:30:33.650 --> 02:30:36.270
Four years after his humiliating defeat,

02:30:36.760 --> 02:30:39.380
the image of Jimmy Carter, the failed president,

02:30:39.880 --> 02:30:41.500
was giving way to Jimmy Carter,

02:30:42.200 --> 02:30:45.010
the committed Christian, in the service of the poor.

02:30:45.630 --> 02:30:48.330
So it is that when we dedicate this center, Mr. President,

02:30:48.850 --> 02:30:52.900
we dedicate an institution that testifies, as does your life itself,

02:30:53.080 --> 02:30:56.950
to the goodness of God and to the blessings he bestows

02:30:56.950 --> 02:31:00.000
upon those who do their best to walk with him.

02:31:00.480 --> 02:31:06.990
In 1986, at the inauguration of the Carter Center, President Reagan expressed the growing

02:31:06.990 --> 02:31:11.010
respect many now felt for the man they had once rejected.

02:31:11.010 --> 02:31:21.160
Through the Carter Center, Jimmy Carter would launch his new career as an elder statesman,

02:31:21.160 --> 02:31:24.100
monitoring elections throughout the world.

02:31:24.100 --> 02:31:30.000
His prestige restored.

02:31:30.000 --> 02:31:34.680
He returned to the role that had given meaning to his presidency, peacemaker.

02:31:37.500 --> 02:31:43.770
Carter has a profound, almost innate commitment to peace.

02:31:44.700 --> 02:31:47.530
It's in his bones.

02:31:49.150 --> 02:31:54.880
He really believes Theodore Roosevelt's adage that one should walk softly and carry a big stick.

02:31:55.000 --> 02:31:59.760
But don't be afraid to walk into the lion's den, and he does that.

02:32:00.160 --> 02:32:03.800
He's done it repeatedly.

02:32:03.800 --> 02:32:08.490
In Haiti, Carter convinced military strongman Raoul Cedris

02:32:08.490 --> 02:32:12.240
to step down in favor of the democratically elected

02:32:12.240 --> 02:32:16.420
president, Jean Bertrand Aristide.

02:32:16.420 --> 02:32:19.940
Carter believes that there's goodness in all people

02:32:19.940 --> 02:32:23.270
and that even the biggest sinner, today's brutal dictator,

02:32:23.270 --> 02:32:25.830
can take Christ into their heart and be born again tomorrow.

02:32:25.830 --> 02:32:27.180
They can be saved.

02:32:27.180 --> 02:32:28.840
We are very glad to be back in Haiti.

02:32:28.840 --> 02:32:30.000
Nous sommes très heureux d'être en France.

02:32:30.000 --> 02:32:36.330
A country obviously dedicated to peace, human rights and democracy.

02:32:36.330 --> 02:32:41.030
So even Cedrus, who was considered a brutal thug by the U.S. government, Carter felt

02:32:41.030 --> 02:32:45.880
that he could appeal to his sense of what is right and what is wrong.

02:32:45.880 --> 02:32:53.520
This caused Carter a lot of criticism, coddling dictators around the world.

02:32:53.520 --> 02:33:00.000
In May 2002, Jimmy Carter went to Cuba, the first American president to visit the island

02:33:00.000 --> 02:33:01.680
in over 40 years.

02:33:15.000 --> 02:33:18.000
In an address broadcast throughout Cuba,

02:33:18.540 --> 02:33:20.840
he defied President George W. Bush

02:33:20.840 --> 02:33:23.100
by calling for an end

02:33:23.100 --> 02:33:25.860
to the decades-old U.S. trade embargo.

02:33:27.320 --> 02:33:30.000
Para establecer relaciones de comercio

02:33:30.000 --> 02:33:43.160
I don't know what's going to happen with the Varela project, which you said was...

02:33:43.160 --> 02:33:49.280
But he also challenged Cuban President Fidel Castro to institute democratic reforms.

02:33:49.280 --> 02:33:59.540
I think it would be very good if your officials would decide to publish the entire document.

02:34:00.320 --> 02:34:02.720
Let there be a free and open debate in Cuba.

02:34:02.720 --> 02:34:12.640
His travels throughout the world, Carter has championed the cause of the poor and the disenfranchised.

02:34:13.410 --> 02:34:22.410
Speaks often eloquently and angrily about the growing gap between rich and poor,

02:34:23.340 --> 02:34:24.680
and between black and white.

02:34:25.590 --> 02:34:30.000
It's very cautious and conservative, fiscally responsible.

02:34:30.030 --> 02:34:37.620
president you'd hear him sometimes now and you think we've got the last socialist in America.

02:34:37.620 --> 02:34:45.560
With the Carter Center, President and Mrs. Carter have created programs to fight disease,

02:34:45.560 --> 02:34:56.120
river blindness, guinea worm, and alleviate hunger. They have so much to give and they feel

02:34:56.120 --> 02:35:00.000
like so many people depend on them. They'll come in and see somebody that has nothing in

02:35:00.000 --> 02:35:08.620
and think hey i can change that life being a good post president doesn't retrospectively make you

02:35:09.120 --> 02:35:15.630
a better president what a post presidency can do though is to illuminate which aspects of a

02:35:15.630 --> 02:35:22.100
president's character were real and which were phony of his strengths perseverance

02:35:22.660 --> 02:35:30.000
dedication integrity those have all turned out to be very very real he never left

02:35:30.000 --> 02:35:32.250
to the American people.

02:35:32.250 --> 02:35:34.550
He kept the peace.

02:35:34.550 --> 02:35:37.850
He brought the hostages home without loss of life,

02:35:37.850 --> 02:35:41.490
all the things that he said that he was going to do.

02:35:41.490 --> 02:35:44.250
It was a time when we needed that sort of person

02:35:44.250 --> 02:35:48.880
as president that people could put some faith and trust in.

02:35:48.880 --> 02:35:50.280
What was Carter?

02:35:50.280 --> 02:35:53.610
He never had a kind of nutshell program.

02:35:53.610 --> 02:35:56.750
He had no interest in either the New Deal tradition

02:35:56.750 --> 02:36:00.000
of Franklin Roosevelt or the New Frontier tradition of John

02:36:00.030 --> 02:36:03.870
Kennedy or the great society of Lyndon Johnson.

02:36:03.870 --> 02:36:07.690
He never crystallized a great agenda of what he wanted to do.

02:36:07.690 --> 02:36:17.250
He simply tackled issues as they confronted him, one by one by one.

02:36:17.250 --> 02:36:22.040
We have not had time to look back and regret things, although I still think the country

02:36:22.040 --> 02:36:24.700
would be better off if Jimmy had been president for another term.

02:36:24.700 --> 02:36:30.000
But then if he hadn't been president for another term, we might not have had the car to sell him.

02:36:30.000 --> 02:36:37.850
at it full-time around the clock with that same dedication that same laser-like concentration

02:36:38.410 --> 02:36:45.030
until finally now the american people are seeing all of this happen and people say hey

02:36:46.280 --> 02:36:53.000
here's really a good man i think history is going to look at him in a kindlier light

02:36:53.000 --> 02:36:59.600
than his contemporaries did his values his devotion to peace and human rights

02:37:00.000 --> 02:37:04.550
keep on resonating in a way that his failures and weaknesses don't.
